WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.00 --> 00:00:01.98
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.98 --> 00:00:02.97
[RUSTLING]

00:00:02.97 --> 00:00:05.94
[CLICKING]

00:00:13.18 --> 00:00:15.60
SARA BEERY: So why
are we all here?

00:00:15.60 --> 00:00:21.20
Deep learning has clearly
been exploding in society.

00:00:21.20 --> 00:00:23.40
Machine learning
generally is something

00:00:23.40 --> 00:00:28.04
that, when I started studying it
about 13 years ago, didn't work,

00:00:28.04 --> 00:00:30.36
and now it works.

00:00:30.36 --> 00:00:35.36
So how many of you here in this
room used AI in the last week?

00:00:35.36 --> 00:00:39.56
Yeah, almost everybody,
probably everybody.

00:00:39.56 --> 00:00:43.92
And we're seeing massive
advances in everything from

00:00:43.92 --> 00:00:49.94
AI-assisted text generation
to 3D reconstruction,

00:00:49.94 --> 00:00:55.46
things like NeRF, things like
AlphaGo, generating images,

00:00:55.46 --> 00:01:00.89
helping with writing
code, playing games.

00:01:00.89 --> 00:01:03.85
This has really started to
touch almost every dimension

00:01:03.85 --> 00:01:08.37
of society, and it's definitely
very exciting to see.

00:01:08.37 --> 00:01:10.09
And I think that
hopefully, all of us

00:01:10.09 --> 00:01:14.57
will learn a lot more about
what that means in this course.

00:01:14.57 --> 00:01:17.01
So what is deep learning?

00:01:17.01 --> 00:01:22.25
First, I would argue that
one necessary component

00:01:22.25 --> 00:01:25.29
of deep learning is the
idea of neural networks.

00:01:25.29 --> 00:01:28.29
And these are a class of
machine learning architectures

00:01:28.29 --> 00:01:31.49
that basically use stacks
of linear transformations

00:01:31.49 --> 00:01:35.57
interleaved with
pointwise non-linearities.

00:01:35.57 --> 00:01:39.33
And these have really
been a building block

00:01:39.33 --> 00:01:43.09
for a lot of the progress
that we saw in the last slide.

00:01:43.09 --> 00:01:45.65
But the other really important
dimension of deep learning

00:01:45.65 --> 00:01:48.25
is the idea of
differential programming.

00:01:48.25 --> 00:01:50.81
And this is essentially
a programming paradigm

00:01:50.81 --> 00:01:53.81
where we parameterize
parts of the program

00:01:53.81 --> 00:01:56.49
and then let
gradient-based optimization

00:01:56.49 --> 00:01:59.51
tune the parameters to
basically optimize that program

00:01:59.51 --> 00:02:04.19
or find some of, at least,
local optimal for that program.

00:02:04.19 --> 00:02:06.51
And these two things
together is what

00:02:06.51 --> 00:02:08.39
we posit make up deep learning.

00:02:08.39 --> 00:02:10.35
And we're really going
to dig into both of them

00:02:10.35 --> 00:02:11.99
in this course.

00:02:11.99 --> 00:02:14.99
So our philosophy for
this is that breakthroughs

00:02:14.99 --> 00:02:18.35
in deep learning have been
driven by a mixture of theory

00:02:18.35 --> 00:02:20.71
and practice, and
both dimensions

00:02:20.71 --> 00:02:24.67
are really vital for future
progress in the field.

00:02:24.67 --> 00:02:27.19
And so along those
lines, this course

00:02:27.19 --> 00:02:30.03
should hopefully provide
both a theoretical grounding

00:02:30.03 --> 00:02:33.11
in important deep
learning building blocks,

00:02:33.11 --> 00:02:37.39
as well as give you practice
implementing, understanding,

00:02:37.39 --> 00:02:40.19
and using those blocks.

00:02:40.19 --> 00:02:42.27
OK, there's still
a lot of people

00:02:42.27 --> 00:02:44.61
in the back who are packed in.

00:02:44.61 --> 00:02:47.51
I don't know if there's any
way to come further forward.

00:02:47.51 --> 00:02:51.15
If not, we'll do our best.

00:02:51.15 --> 00:02:56.11
So the class's coursework is
going to be 65% problem sets.

00:02:56.11 --> 00:02:57.29
You'll have five psets.

00:02:57.29 --> 00:02:59.92
Each will be about
one to two weeks long.

00:02:59.92 --> 00:03:02.30
And then there will be some
combination of pen and paper

00:03:02.30 --> 00:03:04.24
or, more realistically,
maybe Overleaf,

00:03:04.24 --> 00:03:07.36
as well as code that you
will need to do and submit.

00:03:07.36 --> 00:03:10.05
And then 35% of the grade
will be a final project.

00:03:10.05 --> 00:03:11.60
And this is a research
project that's

00:03:11.60 --> 00:03:13.88
focused on deeper understanding
of some of the topics

00:03:13.88 --> 00:03:15.92
that we cover in the course.

00:03:15.92 --> 00:03:18.94
You'll be asked to propose
something for that project.

00:03:18.94 --> 00:03:21.64
So there'll be an
initial project proposal.

00:03:21.64 --> 00:03:24.20
And then the final project
will be a blog post

00:03:24.20 --> 00:03:26.36
that's going to demonstrate
novel experimentation

00:03:26.36 --> 00:03:27.68
and visualization.

00:03:27.68 --> 00:03:29.92
And the hope here is
that I don't know,

00:03:29.92 --> 00:03:32.76
many of you-- how many of you
are machine learning researchers

00:03:32.76 --> 00:03:35.16
who have already written
a machine learning paper,

00:03:35.16 --> 00:03:37.58
as first author, or
anywhere on the author list?

00:03:37.58 --> 00:03:39.41
OK, so some.

00:03:39.41 --> 00:03:41.58
These days, when you write
a machine learning paper,

00:03:41.58 --> 00:03:43.04
more often than
not, you also have

00:03:43.04 --> 00:03:45.32
to write a blog post
about that paper.

00:03:45.32 --> 00:03:47.64
And that's intended
to be catchy, make

00:03:47.64 --> 00:03:52.02
people understand the material,
but really get them engaged.

00:03:52.02 --> 00:03:54.12
And so one of the
reasons that we have this

00:03:54.12 --> 00:03:55.88
as the format of
our final project

00:03:55.88 --> 00:03:59.25
is to reflect on the fact that
this is the reality we live

00:03:59.25 --> 00:04:01.31
in today, that in machine
learning research,

00:04:01.31 --> 00:04:05.45
being able to write a blog-style
description of the work

00:04:05.45 --> 00:04:07.61
that you're doing
and not reduce any

00:04:07.61 --> 00:04:09.97
of the technical complexity
or the information

00:04:09.97 --> 00:04:13.13
that's given across, but
just do this in this slightly

00:04:13.13 --> 00:04:15.81
more visual and maybe
even interactive format is

00:04:15.81 --> 00:04:20.25
a really valuable skill, and one
that we hope this can help us

00:04:20.25 --> 00:04:21.17
think about.

00:04:21.17 --> 00:04:25.85
And we'll allow groups,
but only up to two.

00:04:25.85 --> 00:04:28.25
And another
important component--

00:04:28.25 --> 00:04:29.99
we are not able to
provide compute.

00:04:29.99 --> 00:04:32.99
We may be able to provide
some very limited compute,

00:04:32.99 --> 00:04:34.57
but that's TBD.

00:04:34.57 --> 00:04:37.01
So do not plan
your final project

00:04:37.01 --> 00:04:39.37
to be something where you
need to get state of the art

00:04:39.37 --> 00:04:44.25
by crunching a huge architecture
on a bunch of huge data sets,

00:04:44.25 --> 00:04:46.01
because it's just
not going to work.

00:04:46.01 --> 00:04:48.41
You're not going to be
able to outcompete OpenAI

00:04:48.41 --> 00:04:50.03
on this research project.

00:04:50.03 --> 00:04:50.53
[LAUGHS]

00:04:50.53 --> 00:04:54.77
So this does not mean you
can't do good research.

00:04:54.77 --> 00:04:56.97
And it's important
to get creative here.

00:04:56.97 --> 00:04:59.19
And I think it's actually
a really valuable skill,

00:04:59.19 --> 00:05:03.03
particularly as certain types of
large-scale deep learning become

00:05:03.03 --> 00:05:06.47
more and more centralized, to
think about how you can still do

00:05:06.47 --> 00:05:09.83
impactful machine learning
research in a way that is not

00:05:09.83 --> 00:05:13.23
just "bigger is better."

00:05:13.23 --> 00:05:18.07
All right, any questions
about coursework or logistics?

00:05:18.07 --> 00:05:18.87
Yeah.

00:05:18.87 --> 00:05:20.79
AUDIENCE: Is the
final project expected

00:05:20.79 --> 00:05:24.80
to be a mix of practical
and theoretical research?

00:05:24.80 --> 00:05:27.39
SARA BEERY: So the question was,
is the final project expected

00:05:27.39 --> 00:05:30.71
to be a mix of practical
and theoretical research?

00:05:30.71 --> 00:05:32.29
I think it depends.

00:05:32.29 --> 00:05:35.59
It depends on what you want to
propose as your final project.

00:05:35.59 --> 00:05:37.39
But I think we're pretty open.

00:05:37.39 --> 00:05:39.61
What we want is for
them to be innovative,

00:05:39.61 --> 00:05:42.41
but that innovation can
be in an applied sense,

00:05:42.41 --> 00:05:44.25
or it can be in a more
theoretical sense.

00:05:44.25 --> 00:05:46.55
OK, so I'm going to do a
really high-level overview

00:05:46.55 --> 00:05:50.67
of the schedule, just to
give you a rough sense.

00:05:50.67 --> 00:05:53.96
So today, we're really covering
basic interest stuff-- course

00:05:53.96 --> 00:05:57.76
overview, a rough introduction
to what neural networks are

00:05:57.76 --> 00:05:59.60
and basic building
blocks, and, particularly

00:05:59.60 --> 00:06:01.05
in the context of
signposting, what

00:06:01.05 --> 00:06:03.56
we expect you've seen before,
because this is not an intro

00:06:03.56 --> 00:06:04.66
to deep learning class.

00:06:04.66 --> 00:06:08.68
This is advanced
graduate-level deep learning.

00:06:08.68 --> 00:06:12.04
And then we're going to
go into how you actually

00:06:12.04 --> 00:06:14.80
train a neural net,
then approximation

00:06:14.80 --> 00:06:17.36
theory, some different
architectures, things

00:06:17.36 --> 00:06:19.96
like grids, graphs.

00:06:19.96 --> 00:06:22.64
Then we'll hear from
Jeremy about scaling rules

00:06:22.64 --> 00:06:23.98
for optimization.

00:06:23.98 --> 00:06:26.72
And then we'll look at
generalization theory, more

00:06:26.72 --> 00:06:29.04
architectures, getting
into transformers.

00:06:29.04 --> 00:06:32.40
We'll do a really fun lecture by
Phil called the hacker's guide

00:06:32.40 --> 00:06:35.40
to deep learning that I
think is quite useful.

00:06:35.40 --> 00:06:37.44
And then we'll
talk about memory.

00:06:37.44 --> 00:06:39.69
And then we'll get into
representation learning.

00:06:39.69 --> 00:06:42.28
So there, we're going to look
at reconstruction based and then

00:06:42.28 --> 00:06:45.56
similarity based and then theory
of representation learning.

00:06:45.56 --> 00:06:48.46
Then we'll have a little bit of
a series on generative models,

00:06:48.46 --> 00:06:51.52
the basics, how representation
learning interacts

00:06:51.52 --> 00:06:53.61
with generative
modeling, thinking

00:06:53.61 --> 00:06:55.44
about conditional models.

00:06:55.44 --> 00:06:57.69
Then we'll talk about
generalization, and particularly

00:06:57.69 --> 00:06:59.81
out-of-distribution
generalization.

00:06:59.81 --> 00:07:01.49
We'll touch on
transfer learning,

00:07:01.49 --> 00:07:04.05
both from a models and
a data perspective.

00:07:04.05 --> 00:07:06.52
We'll hear about
large language models.

00:07:06.52 --> 00:07:09.15
We'll have some guest lectures
towards the end of the semester,

00:07:09.15 --> 00:07:10.97
and those are TBD.

00:07:10.97 --> 00:07:14.01
Then we'll hear about scaling
laws, automatic gradient

00:07:14.01 --> 00:07:16.06
descent, and potentially
the past and future

00:07:16.06 --> 00:07:17.73
of deep learning,
though I think there's

00:07:17.73 --> 00:07:19.73
some discussion as to
whether that's going

00:07:19.73 --> 00:07:22.13
to be a different lecture.

00:07:22.13 --> 00:07:24.41
And then, I think
kind of importantly,

00:07:24.41 --> 00:07:28.10
right towards the end, we will
have class-wide project office

00:07:28.10 --> 00:07:29.81
hours where we'll have
a bunch of the TAs

00:07:29.81 --> 00:07:31.49
and the instructors
all come, and we

00:07:31.49 --> 00:07:36.13
can do open discussion around
projects before they're due.

00:07:36.13 --> 00:07:40.33
So the next thing is that we're
going to host PyTorch tutorials.

00:07:40.33 --> 00:07:43.05
This is intended for if you're
not familiar with PyTorch,

00:07:43.05 --> 00:07:44.83
and you think you
need a refresher.

00:07:44.83 --> 00:07:47.95
And we strongly recommend
that if this is the case,

00:07:47.95 --> 00:07:50.37
or if you even think it might
be the case, that you attend

00:07:50.37 --> 00:07:53.07
one of the two PyTorch tutorials
we're going to offer next week.

00:07:53.07 --> 00:07:54.99
So the question is if
PyTorch is a requirement,

00:07:54.99 --> 00:07:56.49
or you're allowed to use
any other frameworks.

00:07:56.49 --> 00:07:58.83
So you're probably welcome
to use any other frameworks

00:07:58.83 --> 00:08:01.15
in your final project, but
some of the problem sets

00:08:01.15 --> 00:08:03.63
specifically will
have code that's

00:08:03.63 --> 00:08:07.03
already built into PyTorch, and
you're filling parts of that in.

00:08:07.03 --> 00:08:08.87
So unless you want
to rewrite all of it

00:08:08.87 --> 00:08:11.85
in JAX or something,
which more power to you,

00:08:11.85 --> 00:08:13.83
I would recommend
that you definitely

00:08:13.83 --> 00:08:17.91
make sure that you are
familiar with PyTorch.

00:08:17.91 --> 00:08:19.64
OK.

00:08:19.64 --> 00:08:21.69
Cool, so I'm going to talk
about course policies.

00:08:21.69 --> 00:08:23.35
And this stuff is important.

00:08:23.35 --> 00:08:25.71
And it's also all
on the web page.

00:08:25.71 --> 00:08:28.59
But I think essentially,
what we want to touch on

00:08:28.59 --> 00:08:31.23
is what our collaboration
policy is first.

00:08:31.23 --> 00:08:34.95
So everyone is required to
write an individual pset.

00:08:34.95 --> 00:08:37.16
So when you're actually
working on your problem sets,

00:08:37.16 --> 00:08:39.03
do not work on something
together and then

00:08:39.03 --> 00:08:41.01
both submit the
exact same thing.

00:08:41.01 --> 00:08:46.17
You can work on the
actual-- the content,

00:08:46.17 --> 00:08:48.45
you can discuss that with
peers, TAs, and instructors,

00:08:48.45 --> 00:08:49.95
but the problem
sets you submit need

00:08:49.95 --> 00:08:53.08
to be your own independent
and individual work.

00:08:53.08 --> 00:08:55.74
And that also applies to
the code that you write.

00:08:55.74 --> 00:08:57.42
So you can discuss
the code with others,

00:08:57.42 --> 00:08:59.86
but you need to each write
your own code separately.

00:08:59.86 --> 00:09:01.86
There was some confusion
about this in the past.

00:09:01.86 --> 00:09:04.76
Just make sure that you're not
taking the code someone else

00:09:04.76 --> 00:09:08.68
wrote, running it, and then
submitting a pset on it.

00:09:08.68 --> 00:09:11.80
You should absolutely not copy
or share complete solutions,

00:09:11.80 --> 00:09:14.64
and you also should not
ask someone else, hey, this

00:09:14.64 --> 00:09:16.64
is my solution, is it right?

00:09:16.64 --> 00:09:18.14
And you shouldn't
do that in person,

00:09:18.14 --> 00:09:20.20
and you also
shouldn't ask, Is this

00:09:20.20 --> 00:09:22.52
correct? on Piazza or Canvas.

00:09:22.52 --> 00:09:24.66
So again, the idea is,
it's your independent work,

00:09:24.66 --> 00:09:26.56
your independent thought.

00:09:26.56 --> 00:09:29.44
And I think it matters more that
you've gone through that process

00:09:29.44 --> 00:09:31.72
independently than
actually getting it correct

00:09:31.72 --> 00:09:34.69
because you'll learn from
the feedback on your psets

00:09:34.69 --> 00:09:37.24
better if you submit something
that you came up with yourself

00:09:37.24 --> 00:09:41.32
and get feedback on it
than if you copy somebody.

00:09:41.32 --> 00:09:44.11
And then if you work with anyone
on the pset other than TAs

00:09:44.11 --> 00:09:46.20
and instructors, we ask
that you write their names

00:09:46.20 --> 00:09:47.10
at the top of the pset.

00:09:47.10 --> 00:09:49.02
So if you have a study group,
you're all working together--

00:09:49.02 --> 00:09:50.60
we don't care if
this is 10 people.

00:09:50.60 --> 00:09:52.96
We don't care if it's, in
this class, 100 people.

00:09:52.96 --> 00:09:55.18
But we just want to know
who was working together.

00:09:55.18 --> 00:09:58.58
I'd be impressed if you managed
to come up with a 100-person

00:09:58.58 --> 00:10:00.98
collaborative group.

00:10:00.98 --> 00:10:06.18
So AI assistance-- we
take the exact same policy

00:10:06.18 --> 00:10:08.90
about things like ChatGPT
or other AI assistants

00:10:08.90 --> 00:10:10.74
that we do with humans.

00:10:10.74 --> 00:10:13.00
So it's a deep learning class.

00:10:13.00 --> 00:10:16.38
You should be trying out
the latest technology.

00:10:16.38 --> 00:10:17.90
The idea is not
that we're teaching

00:10:17.90 --> 00:10:20.54
you to ignore what's happening.

00:10:20.54 --> 00:10:25.32
But we do want you to treat
them like a human collaborator.

00:10:25.32 --> 00:10:27.72
Don't ask them to answer
the question for you.

00:10:27.72 --> 00:10:31.02
Just try to imagine from
an ethics perspective

00:10:31.02 --> 00:10:33.46
that this is like a
peer in the class.

00:10:33.46 --> 00:10:35.40
So don't say, what is
the solution to this?

00:10:35.40 --> 00:10:36.94
Don't say, write
the code for me,

00:10:36.94 --> 00:10:38.52
because you wouldn't
go to your friend

00:10:38.52 --> 00:10:41.14
and say, please
write my code for me.

00:10:41.14 --> 00:10:45.84
But you're super welcome to use
them as a discussion partner.

00:10:45.84 --> 00:10:49.00
Ask them questions
that are contextual.

00:10:49.00 --> 00:10:50.29
Go back and forth.

00:10:50.29 --> 00:10:51.07
That's all fine.

00:10:51.07 --> 00:10:56.15
But don't treat AI
collaboration as if they're not

00:10:56.15 --> 00:10:57.35
a human collaborator.

00:10:57.35 --> 00:10:58.67
Think of it that way first.

00:10:58.67 --> 00:11:02.39
Is this something I would
ask my friend in the class?

00:11:02.39 --> 00:11:04.35
And then just like you
can come to office hours

00:11:04.35 --> 00:11:06.85
and ask a human questions
about the lecture

00:11:06.85 --> 00:11:09.03
material, clarifications
about questions, tips

00:11:09.03 --> 00:11:11.31
for getting started, you're
welcome to do the same

00:11:11.31 --> 00:11:13.59
with AI assistants.

00:11:13.59 --> 00:11:16.15
Again, you're not allowed
to ask an expert friend

00:11:16.15 --> 00:11:17.43
to do your homework for you.

00:11:17.43 --> 00:11:21.11
Don't ask AI to do
your homework for you.

00:11:21.11 --> 00:11:24.47
If you're ever unclear,
imagine the AI is a human

00:11:24.47 --> 00:11:26.51
and apply that same norm.

00:11:26.51 --> 00:11:30.45
And if you work with any AI on
a pset, similarly to the fact

00:11:30.45 --> 00:11:31.83
that we're writing
with humans we

00:11:31.83 --> 00:11:34.67
collaborated with, please
write down which AI

00:11:34.67 --> 00:11:36.31
and how you used it.

00:11:36.31 --> 00:11:38.89
And, of course, this is
a bit vague, but again,

00:11:38.89 --> 00:11:41.79
just kind of honor code.

00:11:41.79 --> 00:11:45.15
Any questions about
AI collaboration,

00:11:45.15 --> 00:11:48.52
or is that reasonably clear?

00:11:48.52 --> 00:11:49.22
Yeah.

00:11:49.22 --> 00:11:51.38
AUDIENCE: I had a
question on the last page.

00:11:51.38 --> 00:11:55.05
So can we show our work
to the TAs to [INAUDIBLE]?

00:11:55.05 --> 00:11:55.72
SARA BEERY: Yes.

00:11:55.72 --> 00:11:58.78
You're very welcome to
show your work to the TAs.

00:11:58.78 --> 00:12:00.16
The TAs are not
going to tell you

00:12:00.16 --> 00:12:01.61
how to answer the
question, though,

00:12:01.61 --> 00:12:05.12
but they will work with you to
help you hopefully figure it out

00:12:05.12 --> 00:12:06.48
on your own.

00:12:06.48 --> 00:12:07.92
Why are we here?

00:12:07.92 --> 00:12:10.88
[LAUGHS] What's the goal?

00:12:10.88 --> 00:12:12.64
So what do we want to do?

00:12:12.64 --> 00:12:17.64
Why are we all interested in
things like deep learning?

00:12:17.64 --> 00:12:20.68
One perspective is that
we're interested in modeling

00:12:20.68 --> 00:12:24.32
complex phenomena
in the real world.

00:12:24.32 --> 00:12:26.40
What are complex phenomena?

00:12:26.40 --> 00:12:28.22
Well, you interact
with them every day,

00:12:28.22 --> 00:12:30.80
things like natural
language, images,

00:12:30.80 --> 00:12:33.56
or visual data in
general, videos.

00:12:33.56 --> 00:12:37.14
DNA, ecosystems are
complex phenomena.

00:12:37.14 --> 00:12:40.28
Climate change is a
complex phenomenon.

00:12:40.28 --> 00:12:44.72
Why is it hard to model complex
phenomena in the real world?

00:12:44.72 --> 00:12:46.32
They're complex.

00:12:46.32 --> 00:12:51.30
[LAUGHS] And so maybe
one existence proof

00:12:51.30 --> 00:12:54.74
for deep learning as a solution
to modeling complex phenomena--

00:12:54.74 --> 00:12:57.16
possibly, we could talk
about the human brain.

00:12:57.16 --> 00:12:59.21
All of you are
here at MIT, which

00:12:59.21 --> 00:13:01.46
means you're pretty good at
modeling complex phenomena

00:13:01.46 --> 00:13:02.88
in your brain, I'm guessing.

00:13:05.66 --> 00:13:10.78
So first, today, I'm going
to just briefly go over

00:13:10.78 --> 00:13:12.24
how we got where we are today.

00:13:12.24 --> 00:13:15.14
This is a brief history
of the field of AI,

00:13:15.14 --> 00:13:17.06
deep learning in general.

00:13:17.06 --> 00:13:19.14
And then I'm going to
go over what we expect

00:13:19.14 --> 00:13:21.10
you have already seen before.

00:13:21.10 --> 00:13:23.24
It's OK if you haven't
seen these things before,

00:13:23.24 --> 00:13:26.50
but we would expect you then
to go and brush up on them.

00:13:26.50 --> 00:13:28.94
So if there's anything that I
talk about, and you're like,

00:13:28.94 --> 00:13:31.64
oh, I have never seen
that, we expect that.

00:13:31.64 --> 00:13:34.70
That's something you can go look
at some of the OpenCourseWare

00:13:34.70 --> 00:13:39.18
courses at MIT, go find lots of
resources online, and pick it up

00:13:39.18 --> 00:13:40.90
before you go into this class.

00:13:40.90 --> 00:13:42.82
And then I'll also talk
about what we actually

00:13:42.82 --> 00:13:45.19
cover in the class.

00:13:45.19 --> 00:13:47.29
So a brief history
of neural networks--

00:13:47.29 --> 00:13:49.83
and I think a fun way
to go through this

00:13:49.83 --> 00:13:52.79
is looking at this on
a plot of enthusiasm

00:13:52.79 --> 00:13:55.03
for neural networks over time.

00:13:55.03 --> 00:13:58.99
So if we go way back
to 1958, Rosenblatt

00:13:58.99 --> 00:14:04.27
introduced the perceptron
in Psychological Review.

00:14:04.27 --> 00:14:09.75
And the perceptron was the
first neural network, arguably.

00:14:09.75 --> 00:14:11.31
And what this was
intending to do

00:14:11.31 --> 00:14:15.79
was recognize or
categorize images.

00:14:15.79 --> 00:14:18.55
And the idea was
essentially that you

00:14:18.55 --> 00:14:22.59
could take some combination
of representations--

00:14:22.59 --> 00:14:25.39
in this case,
particularly pixels--

00:14:25.39 --> 00:14:28.31
sum them, put them through
some non-linearity,

00:14:28.31 --> 00:14:30.91
and get out a categorization.

00:14:30.91 --> 00:14:32.99
And really, we'll see
throughout the course

00:14:32.99 --> 00:14:37.43
and even today, this idea
still forms the building block

00:14:37.43 --> 00:14:39.61
for most of the deep
learning that we do.

00:14:39.61 --> 00:14:42.83
And these perceptrons
are components

00:14:42.83 --> 00:14:44.80
in many, many, many
different types

00:14:44.80 --> 00:14:48.00
of capacities within
things like ChatGPT.

00:14:48.00 --> 00:14:50.74
When this paper came
out back in 1958,

00:14:50.74 --> 00:14:52.68
people were incredibly
enthusiastic.

00:14:52.68 --> 00:14:55.80
It made waves.

00:14:55.80 --> 00:15:00.00
And then in 1972,
Minsky and Papert

00:15:00.00 --> 00:15:03.88
wrote a book called
Perceptrons, Expanded Edition.

00:15:03.88 --> 00:15:07.28
And this book takes a very
critical lens to the idea

00:15:07.28 --> 00:15:09.48
that a perceptron could
be seriously considered

00:15:09.48 --> 00:15:10.88
a model of the brain.

00:15:10.88 --> 00:15:14.56
And it also carefully and
mathematically describes

00:15:14.56 --> 00:15:16.72
and characterizes
the limitations

00:15:16.72 --> 00:15:19.28
of what a perceptron can model.

00:15:19.28 --> 00:15:22.60
And it did so, so
carefully and so critically

00:15:22.60 --> 00:15:26.40
that all of a sudden, enthusiasm
for machine learning really

00:15:26.40 --> 00:15:27.68
took a dip.

00:15:27.68 --> 00:15:32.72
So in the '70s, we were not
excited about neural networks.

00:15:32.72 --> 00:15:37.88
But then in 1986, this book came
out called Parallel Distributed

00:15:37.88 --> 00:15:38.84
Processing.

00:15:38.84 --> 00:15:42.16
And this was fundamental
because it introduces

00:15:42.16 --> 00:15:45.10
the concept of backpropagation.

00:15:45.10 --> 00:15:47.02
And this was really
the thing that

00:15:47.02 --> 00:15:49.78
enabled multilayer
perceptrons, so not just

00:15:49.78 --> 00:15:51.46
a single perceptron,
but actually

00:15:51.46 --> 00:15:54.54
multiple perceptrons
stacked on top of each other

00:15:54.54 --> 00:15:57.86
to actually be reasonably
trained, reasonably fit,

00:15:57.86 --> 00:16:00.18
this concept of being
able to backpropagate

00:16:00.18 --> 00:16:03.58
a gradient through multiple
layers of perceptrons.

00:16:03.58 --> 00:16:05.46
And what this
enabled you to solve

00:16:05.46 --> 00:16:07.79
were things like
the XOR problem.

00:16:07.79 --> 00:16:09.46
For the first time,
you could reasonably

00:16:09.46 --> 00:16:11.14
solve categorization
problems that

00:16:11.14 --> 00:16:14.66
were not separable from
a linear classifier.

00:16:14.66 --> 00:16:16.98
They pointed to backpropagation
as a breakthrough.

00:16:16.98 --> 00:16:20.58
It led us actually
handle things where

00:16:20.58 --> 00:16:25.82
you need multiple different
boundaries and these nonlinear

00:16:25.82 --> 00:16:28.58
boundaries to be drawn,
which is only possible if you

00:16:28.58 --> 00:16:30.54
have multiple layers
of a perceptron.

00:16:30.54 --> 00:16:32.82
You'll never be able
to solve the XOR

00:16:32.82 --> 00:16:36.62
problem with a single-layer
neural network.

00:16:36.62 --> 00:16:38.86
And everyone got
really excited again.

00:16:38.86 --> 00:16:42.57
So in the '80s, yet again,
machine learning was something

00:16:42.57 --> 00:16:44.95
that people were pumped about,
and particularly this deep

00:16:44.95 --> 00:16:48.19
learning neural network
perspective on machine learning.

00:16:48.19 --> 00:16:54.15
In 1998, Yann LeCun put out
convolutional neural networks.

00:16:54.15 --> 00:16:56.83
There's an awesome demo
that he has on his web page.

00:16:56.83 --> 00:17:00.27
I'm sure many of you
have heard of Yann LeCun.

00:17:00.27 --> 00:17:04.39
And this seems
like it would mean

00:17:04.39 --> 00:17:07.75
that everyone was really excited
about neural networks again.

00:17:07.75 --> 00:17:11.06
But if you go to
NeurIPS in 2000,

00:17:11.06 --> 00:17:14.31
this is the premier conference
on machine learning.

00:17:14.31 --> 00:17:16.39
It evolved from an
interdisciplinary conference,

00:17:16.39 --> 00:17:22.47
which was on basically
the science of the brain

00:17:22.47 --> 00:17:25.71
and machine learning to
specifically just a machine

00:17:25.71 --> 00:17:27.10
learning conference.

00:17:27.10 --> 00:17:31.51
If you look at that
conference in 2000,

00:17:31.51 --> 00:17:35.03
the most predictive words
in papers in the title

00:17:35.03 --> 00:17:39.55
for acceptance were "belief
propagation" and "Gaussian."

00:17:39.55 --> 00:17:43.08
And the title words most
predictive of paper rejection

00:17:43.08 --> 00:17:45.72
were "neural" and "network."

00:17:45.72 --> 00:17:47.08
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:47.08 --> 00:17:49.94
So this is what we
called the AI winter.

00:17:49.94 --> 00:17:51.44
And one of the
reasons it was called

00:17:51.44 --> 00:17:54.06
the AI winter is because even
though we had the theory,

00:17:54.06 --> 00:17:56.52
we had the building
blocks, we didn't actually

00:17:56.52 --> 00:18:01.96
have the ability to
train them efficiently.

00:18:01.96 --> 00:18:04.56
We didn't have the right
programming perspective,

00:18:04.56 --> 00:18:07.68
and we didn't have
the right hardware.

00:18:07.68 --> 00:18:12.80
But then in 2012, Alex
Krizhevsky and his co-authors

00:18:12.80 --> 00:18:14.36
published AlexNet.

00:18:14.36 --> 00:18:20.04
And AlexNet was also another
convolutional neural network.

00:18:20.04 --> 00:18:24.60
But importantly, Alex Krizhevsky
was a brilliant programmer,

00:18:24.60 --> 00:18:26.32
and Alex Krizhevsky
figured out how

00:18:26.32 --> 00:18:29.24
to program GPUs, graphics
processing units, that

00:18:29.24 --> 00:18:33.12
have been developed to
handle massive-scale parallel

00:18:33.12 --> 00:18:37.96
multiplications for
graphics, for video games,

00:18:37.96 --> 00:18:44.10
and for high-resolution images.

00:18:44.10 --> 00:18:46.46
But he figured out how to
program those and repurposed

00:18:46.46 --> 00:18:50.22
that hardware for the
training of neural networks.

00:18:50.22 --> 00:18:53.66
And I can't tell you how much
of a shot heard around the world

00:18:53.66 --> 00:18:55.54
this was in our field.

00:18:55.54 --> 00:18:59.94
But this, for the first
time, outperformed

00:18:59.94 --> 00:19:03.42
every single other
possible method

00:19:03.42 --> 00:19:06.78
that was out there on ImageNet.

00:19:06.78 --> 00:19:09.38
ImageNet-- how many of you
have heard of ImageNet?

00:19:09.38 --> 00:19:10.34
Yeah.

00:19:10.34 --> 00:19:12.80
ImageNet was the first
very large-scale data

00:19:12.80 --> 00:19:14.06
set of its kind.

00:19:14.06 --> 00:19:16.18
And I actually
think that this is

00:19:16.18 --> 00:19:18.26
an important
component, what made

00:19:18.26 --> 00:19:20.14
machine learning start to work.

00:19:20.14 --> 00:19:22.16
And I would argue
that, of course,

00:19:22.16 --> 00:19:25.76
it was the theory
and the programming,

00:19:25.76 --> 00:19:29.30
the ability to actually
efficiently fit these things.

00:19:29.30 --> 00:19:33.90
But the other really vital
component was the data.

00:19:33.90 --> 00:19:37.36
Machine learning does not work
without large-scale, curated,

00:19:37.36 --> 00:19:40.60
labeled data in many
capacities still,

00:19:40.60 --> 00:19:42.19
though we're getting
better and better

00:19:42.19 --> 00:19:44.15
at self-supervised learning.

00:19:44.15 --> 00:19:48.91
But so all of a
sudden, we were back.

00:19:48.91 --> 00:19:50.45
Everyone was enthusiastic again.

00:19:50.45 --> 00:19:52.47
And actually, if
you look, these were

00:19:52.47 --> 00:19:56.11
hype cycles of about
28 years, both of them.

00:19:56.11 --> 00:20:00.99
And so the question is, where
are we going to be in 2028?

00:20:00.99 --> 00:20:04.35
So it's 2024 now.

00:20:04.35 --> 00:20:08.03
It's 2024, so in four years,
where are we going to be?

00:20:08.03 --> 00:20:11.61
So one argument would be, OK,
we're in another hype cycle.

00:20:11.61 --> 00:20:15.03
But I would actually argue
that that's probably not true.

00:20:15.03 --> 00:20:17.99
Another one would be
that we're just way

00:20:17.99 --> 00:20:19.85
off the deep end in
terms of enthusiasm,

00:20:19.85 --> 00:20:23.83
and by 2028, we'll be in
some-- out in the stratosphere.

00:20:23.83 --> 00:20:26.71
Or possibly we're doing
something like this.

00:20:26.71 --> 00:20:28.43
Maybe in 2028,
we're going to hit

00:20:28.43 --> 00:20:31.45
some new plane of
enthusiasm, and then

00:20:31.45 --> 00:20:32.91
we'll start to
realize limitations,

00:20:32.91 --> 00:20:34.35
and we'll oscillate again.

00:20:34.35 --> 00:20:35.69
Yeah?

00:20:35.69 --> 00:20:38.34
AUDIENCE: Could AI take
over society by 2028?

00:20:38.34 --> 00:20:39.84
SARA BEERY: [LAUGHS]
So the question

00:20:39.84 --> 00:20:42.64
is, will AI take
over society by 2028?

00:20:42.64 --> 00:20:44.56
My answer is, let's see.

00:20:44.56 --> 00:20:46.16
[LAUGHS]

00:20:46.16 --> 00:20:47.80
So what's deep learning today?

00:20:47.80 --> 00:20:51.56
What are the components
that make it up?

00:20:51.56 --> 00:20:55.08
First, I think a really
important one is Autograd.

00:20:55.08 --> 00:20:58.44
These are coding languages
like PyTorch and TensorFlow

00:20:58.44 --> 00:21:00.32
that give us a really
clever way to do things

00:21:00.32 --> 00:21:04.04
like implement the chain
rule in software, making

00:21:04.04 --> 00:21:06.48
good use of available hardware.

00:21:06.48 --> 00:21:09.42
And this has enabled
us to do things

00:21:09.42 --> 00:21:11.94
like approximate the
gradient of any function,

00:21:11.94 --> 00:21:14.08
even if it's an approximation.

00:21:14.08 --> 00:21:17.14
These are a really important
component of deep learning.

00:21:17.14 --> 00:21:19.38
And if you do anything
with deep learning,

00:21:19.38 --> 00:21:21.30
I doubt that you're doing
what Krizhevsky did.

00:21:21.30 --> 00:21:23.26
I doubt that you're programming
these things from scratch.

00:21:23.26 --> 00:21:25.63
You're almost certainly working
with existing programming

00:21:25.63 --> 00:21:28.64
languages that are built
for machine learning.

00:21:28.64 --> 00:21:32.67
Its billion-plus data point
datasets, things like LAION.

00:21:32.67 --> 00:21:34.72
So that scale of data that
I talked about before,

00:21:34.72 --> 00:21:37.74
this is an incredibly important
component of all of this.

00:21:37.74 --> 00:21:41.82
Parallel training on
thousands of GPUs--

00:21:41.82 --> 00:21:45.06
again, this is what
we're looking at today--

00:21:45.06 --> 00:21:46.92
billion-plus parameter
architectures,

00:21:46.92 --> 00:21:51.02
and this number just keeps
increasing, million-plus

00:21:51.02 --> 00:21:52.90
dollar training costs.

00:21:52.90 --> 00:21:55.30
So, of course, it's
not public knowledge

00:21:55.30 --> 00:21:57.98
how much it costs to train
something like GPT 4.0.

00:21:57.98 --> 00:22:03.90
But I would bet it's definitely
in the many millions.

00:22:03.90 --> 00:22:06.46
Shockingly good results-- I
don't know about any of you.

00:22:06.46 --> 00:22:08.80
I mean, I've been working in
this field now for a while,

00:22:08.80 --> 00:22:13.38
and I think we've been seeing
results recently that I didn't

00:22:13.38 --> 00:22:16.86
anticipate seeing in my lifetime
from when I started, back when

00:22:16.86 --> 00:22:18.38
things didn't work.

00:22:18.38 --> 00:22:20.36
So that's been both
exciting to see,

00:22:20.36 --> 00:22:23.37
and also sometimes, I think, for
many of us a bit overwhelming,

00:22:23.37 --> 00:22:25.58
because you're like, oh,
gosh, the problems I thought

00:22:25.58 --> 00:22:27.54
were problems aren't
problems anymore.

00:22:27.54 --> 00:22:29.58
So what are the problems?

00:22:29.58 --> 00:22:33.18
Of course, I work
in the world of--

00:22:33.18 --> 00:22:36.71
I work a lot on
biodiversity loss

00:22:36.71 --> 00:22:38.42
at a global scale
using machine learning.

00:22:38.42 --> 00:22:40.59
And I would say that
biodiversity loss is definitely

00:22:40.59 --> 00:22:43.04
still a problem, and one that
machine learning is sometimes

00:22:43.04 --> 00:22:46.63
helping, but often also
hurting, because the carbon

00:22:46.63 --> 00:22:52.03
costs of actually training
these models is significant.

00:22:52.03 --> 00:22:56.25
But all of these
massive things-- massive

00:22:56.25 --> 00:22:57.31
isn't necessary.

00:22:57.31 --> 00:22:59.95
So things like Stable Diffusion
are actually pretty lightweight

00:22:59.95 --> 00:23:01.89
and were incredibly impactful.

00:23:01.89 --> 00:23:06.15
So things don't always
need to be big to be good.

00:23:06.15 --> 00:23:08.39
And then I think another
really important component

00:23:08.39 --> 00:23:11.51
of deep learning today is
the idea that it is primarily

00:23:11.51 --> 00:23:13.59
an open source
community where we have

00:23:13.59 --> 00:23:15.99
what we call modular reuse.

00:23:15.99 --> 00:23:19.75
So people take weights that were
trained by one person with one

00:23:19.75 --> 00:23:20.37
architecture.

00:23:20.37 --> 00:23:22.87
They might take that and use
that entire thing as a module

00:23:22.87 --> 00:23:24.99
within another,
larger system that

00:23:24.99 --> 00:23:27.23
has many different
components that are being

00:23:27.23 --> 00:23:29.67
pulled from different things.

00:23:29.67 --> 00:23:32.44
Now, I think that this open
source perspective is really

00:23:32.44 --> 00:23:35.03
something that's driven a lot
of progress in machine learning.

00:23:35.03 --> 00:23:36.44
But also one of
the things that we

00:23:36.44 --> 00:23:38.85
have been seeing in
the last few years

00:23:38.85 --> 00:23:42.65
is that things are less and
less likely to be open source.

00:23:42.65 --> 00:23:45.13
You might have an API,
but you aren't necessarily

00:23:45.13 --> 00:23:47.93
going to get the weights and
the architecture for something

00:23:47.93 --> 00:23:50.77
like GPT 4.0.

00:23:50.77 --> 00:23:53.41
So for the rest of
the lecture, this

00:23:53.41 --> 00:23:55.57
is the signposting
I'm going to use.

00:23:55.57 --> 00:23:58.79
Looking back, this is what
we expect you've seen before.

00:23:58.79 --> 00:24:00.17
The green looking
forward, that's

00:24:00.17 --> 00:24:02.17
what we're going to
cover in this class.

00:24:02.17 --> 00:24:05.17
All right, pretty
straightforward.

00:24:05.17 --> 00:24:07.33
I expect you have
seen gradient descent

00:24:07.33 --> 00:24:09.97
before you come to this class.

00:24:09.97 --> 00:24:13.45
So gradient descent
essentially is the idea

00:24:13.45 --> 00:24:17.93
that we're trying to
optimize some cost function.

00:24:17.93 --> 00:24:19.73
And the way we're going
to do that is we're

00:24:19.73 --> 00:24:24.29
finding some minimum over a set
of all of our training data,

00:24:24.29 --> 00:24:27.65
some minimum set of parameters,
where then the loss is actually

00:24:27.65 --> 00:24:30.53
minimized for all of those
different data points.

00:24:30.53 --> 00:24:33.22
So what this actually
looks like in practice,

00:24:33.22 --> 00:24:35.30
you're trying to find
some theta star, which

00:24:35.30 --> 00:24:37.62
is like your optimal
set of model parameters

00:24:37.62 --> 00:24:39.32
that minimize your
cost function.

00:24:39.32 --> 00:24:41.62
And so maybe you start
somewhere random,

00:24:41.62 --> 00:24:44.90
and then you calculate
your gradient.

00:24:44.90 --> 00:24:47.82
Now you update the
weights of that model

00:24:47.82 --> 00:24:50.84
in the direction of the
gradient, and you step through,

00:24:50.84 --> 00:24:53.18
et cetera, et cetera.

00:24:53.18 --> 00:24:56.62
And I think many of you have
probably seen this before,

00:24:56.62 --> 00:24:59.02
and we'll talk in
an upcoming lecture,

00:24:59.02 --> 00:25:01.02
cover some of the different
dimensions of things

00:25:01.02 --> 00:25:03.94
like stochastic gradient
descent, et cetera.

00:25:03.94 --> 00:25:08.26
But we're assuming that
you've seen this idea before,

00:25:08.26 --> 00:25:12.06
probably in intro to ML
lecture, for example.

00:25:12.06 --> 00:25:15.18
Covering in this class, we'll go
into a little bit more detail,

00:25:15.18 --> 00:25:17.06
backprop and
specifically this idea

00:25:17.06 --> 00:25:19.54
of differential programming.

00:25:19.54 --> 00:25:21.98
So what does it actually look
like to build programming

00:25:21.98 --> 00:25:26.58
languages that are structured
around the idea of optimality

00:25:26.58 --> 00:25:29.78
and optimizing through gradient
descent for different components

00:25:29.78 --> 00:25:32.03
of that architecture?

00:25:32.03 --> 00:25:33.91
And I think one of
the things that's fun

00:25:33.91 --> 00:25:37.47
is thinking about what are
you actually optimizing for,

00:25:37.47 --> 00:25:42.15
and what are you pushing
the gradients through to?

00:25:42.15 --> 00:25:47.91
So here, one iteration
of gradient descent

00:25:47.91 --> 00:25:50.59
is essentially
something like this,

00:25:50.59 --> 00:25:54.91
and we have some learning rate.

00:25:54.91 --> 00:25:57.59
And all of that, we're going
to go into in a lot more detail

00:25:57.59 --> 00:26:00.43
next Tuesday where we're
going to be talking about,

00:26:00.43 --> 00:26:02.23
where does this
actually come through,

00:26:02.23 --> 00:26:05.63
how do we think about what
some of these choices are,

00:26:05.63 --> 00:26:08.95
and how we actually
build out the learning

00:26:08.95 --> 00:26:11.59
structure for backprop.

00:26:11.59 --> 00:26:13.19
I do expect that
you've seen things

00:26:13.19 --> 00:26:16.11
like multilayer perceptrons
and nonlinearities, things

00:26:16.11 --> 00:26:18.43
like ReLUs, before.

00:26:18.43 --> 00:26:21.71
So computational neural network
is often some form of vector

00:26:21.71 --> 00:26:23.67
in, vector out.

00:26:23.67 --> 00:26:25.99
And this arrow that goes
from vector in to vector

00:26:25.99 --> 00:26:27.87
out is a lot of what
we're going to be

00:26:27.87 --> 00:26:30.37
talking about in this class.

00:26:30.37 --> 00:26:33.57
What computation
does that cover?

00:26:33.57 --> 00:26:35.07
How does that
actually build out?

00:26:35.07 --> 00:26:36.69
So one cool thing
about neural networks

00:26:36.69 --> 00:26:40.49
is that they often are reusing
the same simple computational

00:26:40.49 --> 00:26:42.61
units over and over again.

00:26:42.61 --> 00:26:46.25
So there's often a lot of
the same kinds of computation

00:26:46.25 --> 00:26:49.15
that are being
repeated and reused.

00:26:49.15 --> 00:26:51.49
And what these actually look
like, these building blocks

00:26:51.49 --> 00:26:54.13
that are being used
over and over again,

00:26:54.13 --> 00:26:57.61
they're often some
combination of a linear layer.

00:26:57.61 --> 00:27:00.25
So here-- and we will be
sticking with this type

00:27:00.25 --> 00:27:02.57
of notation for the
rest of the class--

00:27:02.57 --> 00:27:05.45
what we call z sub j, so
some component of this output

00:27:05.45 --> 00:27:08.57
representation in
that linear layer,

00:27:08.57 --> 00:27:13.49
that would be a sum over i
of some weight times each

00:27:13.49 --> 00:27:18.57
of those input components--
basically, pretty simple.

00:27:18.57 --> 00:27:21.50
And that can also be written
essentially this way, where

00:27:21.50 --> 00:27:23.09
you have some set
of weights, and then

00:27:23.09 --> 00:27:25.11
you also incorporate
a bias term.

00:27:25.11 --> 00:27:27.21
And this is a term
that does not actually

00:27:27.21 --> 00:27:29.16
take in any of those
input components.

00:27:29.16 --> 00:27:31.50
It's just a bias.

00:27:31.50 --> 00:27:34.78
And that can also be written
in a vectorized notation, which

00:27:34.78 --> 00:27:36.58
makes everything
much more simple.

00:27:36.58 --> 00:27:41.14
So again, just with a focus on
the notation, that output unit z

00:27:41.14 --> 00:27:46.10
sub j is x, that input
vector, transposed times

00:27:46.10 --> 00:27:49.22
w sub j, so the set of weights
that correspond to that output

00:27:49.22 --> 00:27:52.58
component and that bias term.

00:27:52.58 --> 00:27:54.70
And then what we
consider theta-- so

00:27:54.70 --> 00:27:56.66
that's all the parameters
of the model-- that's

00:27:56.66 --> 00:28:01.14
the set of all the weight
terms and all the bias terms.

00:28:01.14 --> 00:28:02.74
So what actually
makes a neural net

00:28:02.74 --> 00:28:04.54
a neural net is the
fact that it's not just

00:28:04.54 --> 00:28:06.62
linear combinations of inputs.

00:28:06.62 --> 00:28:10.86
You also have some sort
of nonlinear function

00:28:10.86 --> 00:28:12.92
that is mapping your outputs.

00:28:12.92 --> 00:28:15.18
And that's something
like g sub z.

00:28:15.18 --> 00:28:16.62
And in this case,
that's something

00:28:16.62 --> 00:28:18.48
like a pointwise non-linearity.

00:28:18.48 --> 00:28:20.94
So that's an important dimension
that these non-linearities

00:28:20.94 --> 00:28:23.14
are usually pointwise.

00:28:23.14 --> 00:28:25.86
So one possible non-linearity
would be this one.

00:28:25.86 --> 00:28:29.99
g sub z would be 1 if z is
greater than 0, or 0 otherwise.

00:28:29.99 --> 00:28:32.65
Is this a good choice of
non-linearity for a neural net?

00:28:32.65 --> 00:28:33.35
AUDIENCE: No.

00:28:33.35 --> 00:28:34.19
SARA BEERY: Why not?

00:28:34.19 --> 00:28:36.03
AUDIENCE: It's not
differentiable?

00:28:36.03 --> 00:28:39.07
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so it's
not differentiable, exactly.

00:28:39.07 --> 00:28:43.89
And why is the fact that it's
not differentiable not good?

00:28:43.89 --> 00:28:44.39
Yeah.

00:28:44.39 --> 00:28:47.35
AUDIENCE: Because it
hinders backpropagation.

00:28:47.35 --> 00:28:49.15
SARA BEERY: It hinders
backpropagation.

00:28:49.15 --> 00:28:51.07
Why?

00:28:51.07 --> 00:28:53.47
AUDIENCE: It makes
the [INAUDIBLE] set

00:28:53.47 --> 00:28:55.43
harder [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:55.43 --> 00:28:57.21
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so
the directionality.

00:28:57.21 --> 00:29:01.75
If you're anywhere
on this graph,

00:29:01.75 --> 00:29:04.55
you don't know which
way to go, essentially.

00:29:04.55 --> 00:29:05.95
The gradient is zero.

00:29:05.95 --> 00:29:08.61
So you can try to take
a gradient ascent step.

00:29:08.61 --> 00:29:13.43
You're not going to move because
the gradient is zero, exactly.

00:29:13.43 --> 00:29:18.87
So this roughly is called a
perceptron, this linear input

00:29:18.87 --> 00:29:22.27
plus a pointwise non-linearity.

00:29:22.27 --> 00:29:26.97
And you actually can arguably
do linear classification

00:29:26.97 --> 00:29:27.95
with a perceptron.

00:29:27.95 --> 00:29:30.73
So if you take a
really simple version

00:29:30.73 --> 00:29:34.33
of this where your input is
just two-dimensional, x1, x2,

00:29:34.33 --> 00:29:37.61
and you have some w1/w2 learned
weight terms that you use

00:29:37.61 --> 00:29:39.91
to combine to get z,
which is your hidden unit,

00:29:39.91 --> 00:29:43.77
and then you map that
via a function to y,

00:29:43.77 --> 00:29:46.77
you then define z as
x transpose w plus b.

00:29:46.77 --> 00:29:51.17
And y is now some
function g of z.

00:29:51.17 --> 00:29:54.57
What this looks like
for any set of values

00:29:54.57 --> 00:29:56.97
will be something like
this, where you essentially

00:29:56.97 --> 00:29:58.61
have almost a ramp.

00:29:58.61 --> 00:30:04.29
It's a clear and
standard gradient across.

00:30:04.29 --> 00:30:06.15
Why would it look like this?

00:30:06.15 --> 00:30:10.75
So why is it smooth and
reasonably straight?

00:30:14.33 --> 00:30:17.89
I maybe didn't pose that
question super clearly.

00:30:17.89 --> 00:30:21.33
So essentially, the
reason that mapping it

00:30:21.33 --> 00:30:24.33
looks like this is
because it's a plane.

00:30:24.33 --> 00:30:28.94
So any two-dimensional
version of this

00:30:28.94 --> 00:30:33.78
is going to be a plane
with some orientation.

00:30:33.78 --> 00:30:36.20
And then very
straightforward, actually,

00:30:36.20 --> 00:30:38.14
to turn that into a
linear classifier,

00:30:38.14 --> 00:30:39.58
because all you're
doing is taking

00:30:39.58 --> 00:30:41.82
some threshold on that plane.

00:30:41.82 --> 00:30:43.90
So even a single-layer
neural network

00:30:43.90 --> 00:30:46.04
can perform linear
classification

00:30:46.04 --> 00:30:47.58
because now this
threshold, that's

00:30:47.58 --> 00:30:50.32
basically what that stepwise
non-linearity is giving you.

00:30:50.32 --> 00:30:55.18
It's giving you something that
is thresholded at some value.

00:30:55.18 --> 00:30:57.16
And so even with
something quite simple,

00:30:57.16 --> 00:30:59.06
you can do classification.

00:30:59.06 --> 00:31:02.65
Now, that's assuming that your
data is linearly separable.

00:31:02.65 --> 00:31:04.28
So if you have training
data like this,

00:31:04.28 --> 00:31:08.34
which is linearly separable,
and now you want to find some

00:31:08.34 --> 00:31:12.02
optimal set of weights and
biases that will minimize some

00:31:12.02 --> 00:31:16.50
loss term where now, again, this
loss is the difference between

00:31:16.50 --> 00:31:19.94
the true value-- so here,
the true value is 1 or 0--

00:31:19.94 --> 00:31:23.62
and the functional mapping
of the inputs to something

00:31:23.62 --> 00:31:25.12
like that value.

00:31:25.12 --> 00:31:26.67
If you have something
like this, this

00:31:26.67 --> 00:31:28.15
is what we'd call a bad fit.

00:31:28.15 --> 00:31:31.03
You have 7 misclassifications.

00:31:31.03 --> 00:31:33.27
You're wrong about more
things than you're right.

00:31:33.27 --> 00:31:35.51
And all of those
red values, those

00:31:35.51 --> 00:31:38.95
are going to give you a high
value in your loss function.

00:31:38.95 --> 00:31:42.55
Or you could find something
that's like an OK fit,

00:31:42.55 --> 00:31:45.09
less misclassifications.

00:31:45.09 --> 00:31:48.03
And maybe this is something
that your model updates to

00:31:48.03 --> 00:31:50.11
after a single gradient step.

00:31:50.11 --> 00:31:53.07
Your decision surface is linear,
and now you're fiddling around

00:31:53.07 --> 00:31:56.99
with what that classifier is--

00:31:56.99 --> 00:32:00.91
basically, which values of
weights and biases you need.

00:32:00.91 --> 00:32:02.42
And then maybe
after a few steps,

00:32:02.42 --> 00:32:04.51
you would find something
that is a good fit, where

00:32:04.51 --> 00:32:07.19
you're not actually
misclassifying anything.

00:32:07.19 --> 00:32:09.59
So it is possible
to do classification

00:32:09.59 --> 00:32:12.21
of linear things,
linearly separable things,

00:32:12.21 --> 00:32:14.31
even with something
like a perceptron that's

00:32:14.31 --> 00:32:16.67
doing these really
simple thresholding-based

00:32:16.67 --> 00:32:19.27
non-linearities.

00:32:19.27 --> 00:32:21.87
But like we said,
this actually would

00:32:21.87 --> 00:32:24.97
be really difficult to learn.

00:32:24.97 --> 00:32:29.85
So instead, maybe you would
try something like a tanh.

00:32:29.85 --> 00:32:33.93
So this is another
pointwise non-linearity.

00:32:33.93 --> 00:32:38.61
And it was one of the earliest
ones that people looked at.

00:32:38.61 --> 00:32:40.13
The things that
are nice about this

00:32:40.13 --> 00:32:42.97
is it's bounded between minus
1 and 1, so none of the values

00:32:42.97 --> 00:32:44.74
are super huge.

00:32:44.74 --> 00:32:46.33
And you get saturation
of the gradient

00:32:46.33 --> 00:32:48.07
for super large or
super small inputs.

00:32:48.07 --> 00:32:51.55
You're not getting
explosions necessarily.

00:32:51.55 --> 00:32:53.45
But also, if you start
out somewhere that's

00:32:53.45 --> 00:32:55.01
really far from the
center, you don't

00:32:55.01 --> 00:32:58.17
have a lot of training signal
because we're getting close to 0

00:32:58.17 --> 00:32:59.73
in our gradient.

00:32:59.73 --> 00:33:01.73
And so those
gradients do go to 0

00:33:01.73 --> 00:33:04.69
as we go to infinity
in either direction.

00:33:04.69 --> 00:33:07.61
The outputs are centered at 0.

00:33:07.61 --> 00:33:12.37
So the tanh of z can also be
written as 2 times the sigmoid

00:33:12.37 --> 00:33:13.89
of 2z minus 1.

00:33:13.89 --> 00:33:16.45
So let's look at what that
sigmoid function looks like.

00:33:16.45 --> 00:33:19.81
So in this case, this
is a sigmoid, 1 over 1

00:33:19.81 --> 00:33:25.09
plus e to the minus
h or e to the minus--

00:33:25.09 --> 00:33:26.40
the notation is wrong.

00:33:26.40 --> 00:33:28.50
It should be minus z.

00:33:28.50 --> 00:33:31.42
This can be interpreted as
the firing rate of a neuron.

00:33:31.42 --> 00:33:34.28
That was one interpretation
of it initially.

00:33:34.28 --> 00:33:38.58
It's bounded between 0 and
1, so it's not ever negative.

00:33:38.58 --> 00:33:40.62
Again, you have saturation
of that gradient

00:33:40.62 --> 00:33:43.46
for super large or
super small inputs.

00:33:43.46 --> 00:33:46.38
And all those gradients go to 0.

00:33:46.38 --> 00:33:48.75
And the outputs themselves
are centered around 0.5.

00:33:48.75 --> 00:33:50.30
So this is kind of
poor conditioning.

00:33:50.30 --> 00:33:52.46
It's slightly biased.

00:33:52.46 --> 00:33:55.14
In practice, we don't
actually use this.

00:33:55.14 --> 00:33:57.66
So instead, in
practice, what we often

00:33:57.66 --> 00:33:59.66
use is something called
a rectified linear unit,

00:33:59.66 --> 00:34:01.06
or a ReLU.

00:34:01.06 --> 00:34:05.62
And this is just
the max of 0 and z.

00:34:05.62 --> 00:34:08.76
And this is unbounded
on the positive side,

00:34:08.76 --> 00:34:12.21
so it can go all the way to
infinity, which can result in,

00:34:12.21 --> 00:34:14.58
for example, sometimes things
like exploding gradients,

00:34:14.58 --> 00:34:16.46
because the values
can get very large.

00:34:16.46 --> 00:34:18.86
But it's super
efficient to implement.

00:34:18.86 --> 00:34:23.07
Essentially, the derivatives
are just that step function.

00:34:23.07 --> 00:34:25.86
And it also seems to help
speed up convergence.

00:34:25.86 --> 00:34:28.11
So in that Krizhevsky paper,
they showed that they got

00:34:28.11 --> 00:34:32.46
something like a 6x speed-up
using a ReLU over using

00:34:32.46 --> 00:34:34.83
something like a tanh in
terms of converging that model

00:34:34.83 --> 00:34:36.11
and learning.

00:34:36.11 --> 00:34:39.05
The drawback is that if you're
strongly in the negative region,

00:34:39.05 --> 00:34:41.42
the unit's what we call dead.

00:34:41.42 --> 00:34:44.42
There is no gradient,
and so you essentially

00:34:44.42 --> 00:34:46.75
have this gradient
collapse, where

00:34:46.75 --> 00:34:51.43
you can no longer learn from
that component of the vector.

00:34:51.43 --> 00:34:53.38
But this is our default
choice, and it's widely

00:34:53.38 --> 00:34:54.71
used in current models.

00:34:54.71 --> 00:34:56.96
There's lots of
slight tweaks to this,

00:34:56.96 --> 00:34:59.87
but this is really,
really common.

00:34:59.87 --> 00:35:02.91
So then the last thing I want
to talk about in this section

00:35:02.91 --> 00:35:04.63
is stacking layers.

00:35:04.63 --> 00:35:06.59
So we talked about
a single version

00:35:06.59 --> 00:35:09.59
of this, where you have
your linear projection,

00:35:09.59 --> 00:35:11.27
and then you have
a non-linearity.

00:35:11.27 --> 00:35:13.07
But now you can take
that, and you can just

00:35:13.07 --> 00:35:14.91
stack another one on top of it.

00:35:14.91 --> 00:35:18.67
So now both z and
h are hidden units.

00:35:18.67 --> 00:35:20.55
They're somewhere in
the middle of the model.

00:35:20.55 --> 00:35:23.01
We don't necessarily--
they're not seen

00:35:23.01 --> 00:35:25.73
at the inputs or the outputs.

00:35:25.73 --> 00:35:27.21
And essentially,
then, you can also

00:35:27.21 --> 00:35:28.85
remember that all
of these things

00:35:28.85 --> 00:35:32.45
are stacked on top of each
other, almost in parallel

00:35:32.45 --> 00:35:34.61
as well, because all of
these inputs are mapping

00:35:34.61 --> 00:35:37.09
to the different components
of the output vector.

00:35:37.09 --> 00:35:40.49
And so you have weights for all
of those different dimensions.

00:35:40.49 --> 00:35:45.01
So this means that h, our
output of the first layer,

00:35:45.01 --> 00:35:48.37
is going to be g, that
pointwise non-linearity, applied

00:35:48.37 --> 00:35:54.05
to now instead of vectorized
w transpose x sub j to give us

00:35:54.05 --> 00:35:57.37
the individual j-th component,
you can actually just think

00:35:57.37 --> 00:35:59.91
of this now as a matrix
multiplied by a vector,

00:35:59.91 --> 00:36:02.81
all of the weights for all
of those different dimensions

00:36:02.81 --> 00:36:06.81
multiplied by the vector x plus
the vector of all your biases,

00:36:06.81 --> 00:36:07.81
b1.

00:36:07.81 --> 00:36:10.69
So now you have this
cleaner notation.

00:36:10.69 --> 00:36:15.45
And then now y is again g, that
same pointwise non-linearity

00:36:15.45 --> 00:36:20.06
applied on top of your
outputs of your first thing,

00:36:20.06 --> 00:36:23.78
but now with h, multiplied
by that vector of all

00:36:23.78 --> 00:36:26.06
your second-layer
weights and added

00:36:26.06 --> 00:36:28.02
with your second-layer
bias term.

00:36:28.02 --> 00:36:31.17
And so now, your set theta,
your model representation,

00:36:31.17 --> 00:36:33.30
is the set of all of the
different weights from all

00:36:33.30 --> 00:36:35.62
the different layers, as well as
all the different biases in all

00:36:35.62 --> 00:36:36.49
the different layers.

00:36:36.49 --> 00:36:37.78
Oh, thank you.

00:36:37.78 --> 00:36:38.28
Oh, yeah.

00:36:38.28 --> 00:36:39.19
Thank you.

00:36:39.19 --> 00:36:40.74
AUDIENCE: Based off
your application,

00:36:40.74 --> 00:36:42.60
how do you choose your
activation function?

00:36:42.60 --> 00:36:44.74
What are the parameters of
what might be considered

00:36:44.74 --> 00:36:49.38
a good model or, I guess,
a good activation function

00:36:49.38 --> 00:36:52.82
based off of what you want
to use your model for?

00:36:52.82 --> 00:36:54.76
SARA BEERY: So you're
asking, in practice,

00:36:54.76 --> 00:36:58.42
are there any best practices for
choosing the non-linearity based

00:36:58.42 --> 00:37:02.10
on different types of data.

00:37:02.10 --> 00:37:03.94
I think in the literature,
there's maybe--

00:37:03.94 --> 00:37:06.12
you can see it coming out.

00:37:06.12 --> 00:37:12.22
But often, people really
do use ReLU quite a lot.

00:37:12.22 --> 00:37:17.15
But I wouldn't say that there's
any great general rules of thumb

00:37:17.15 --> 00:37:17.71
there.

00:37:17.71 --> 00:37:25.69
And often-- yeah.

00:37:25.69 --> 00:37:27.19
I would say there's
different things

00:37:27.19 --> 00:37:30.65
that people have converged upon
based on experimental success.

00:37:30.65 --> 00:37:33.71
So you'll see in the
literature like, oh, yeah,

00:37:33.71 --> 00:37:35.59
everything in this line
of work on these data

00:37:35.59 --> 00:37:38.35
sets with these things,
they're always using

00:37:38.35 --> 00:37:40.65
one or the other component.

00:37:40.65 --> 00:37:42.25
And then people build on that.

00:37:42.25 --> 00:37:44.69
Sometimes that's called grad
student gradient descent

00:37:44.69 --> 00:37:47.31
[LAUGHS] because
you're essentially

00:37:47.31 --> 00:37:49.79
writing research papers and
learning from those research

00:37:49.79 --> 00:37:53.11
papers what parameters in terms
of things like your activation

00:37:53.11 --> 00:37:54.17
functions make sense.

00:37:54.17 --> 00:37:57.83
But yeah, I don't know that
I have great rules of thumb

00:37:57.83 --> 00:37:59.47
of, oh, for this
type of application,

00:37:59.47 --> 00:38:02.79
you should always
use this or that.

00:38:02.79 --> 00:38:03.95
Do you?

00:38:03.95 --> 00:38:05.71
AUDIENCE: Yeah, it's
not a hard science.

00:38:05.71 --> 00:38:06.60
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

00:38:06.60 --> 00:38:08.15
AUDIENCE: But it's
usually about what

00:38:08.15 --> 00:38:10.91
makes the network more trainable
rather than trying to model

00:38:10.91 --> 00:38:12.55
a certain kind of data.

00:38:12.55 --> 00:38:14.63
There are a couple of
examples, like using

00:38:14.63 --> 00:38:18.21
a sinusoidal non-linearity
to pick out some for you,

00:38:18.21 --> 00:38:19.13
if you'd like.

00:38:19.13 --> 00:38:21.49
There are a few examples
that do but usually not.

00:38:21.49 --> 00:38:25.45
And [INAUDIBLE] does deep
learning has a great answer

00:38:25.45 --> 00:38:26.26
to that question.

00:38:26.26 --> 00:38:26.97
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

00:38:26.97 --> 00:38:28.37
So what Jeremy was
saying is basically,

00:38:28.37 --> 00:38:29.47
it's not a hard science.

00:38:29.47 --> 00:38:31.45
We don't have
theoretical guarantees

00:38:31.45 --> 00:38:34.25
of this is what you should do.

00:38:34.25 --> 00:38:36.19
But in practice,
there are-- yeah,

00:38:36.19 --> 00:38:38.32
there's counterexamples to that.

00:38:38.32 --> 00:38:40.45
So if you know, for example,
that your features are

00:38:40.45 --> 00:38:43.37
sinusoidal, it maybe makes
sense to try to pick out

00:38:43.37 --> 00:38:44.63
Fourier representations.

00:38:44.63 --> 00:38:46.13
You're working in
the Fourier space,

00:38:46.13 --> 00:38:49.53
then maybe you should use
a sinusoidal activation.

00:38:49.53 --> 00:38:52.71
Anyway, but yes, I wish that
we better understood it.

00:38:52.71 --> 00:38:55.65
And that's, I think, why
we need a lot more research

00:38:55.65 --> 00:38:57.61
on the theoretical
side of understanding,

00:38:57.61 --> 00:39:02.21
how do we actually choose
these things appropriately

00:39:02.21 --> 00:39:04.57
instead of maybe learning
everything from scratch

00:39:04.57 --> 00:39:07.71
or doing it experimentally,
burning all those trees?

00:39:10.59 --> 00:39:11.09
Cool.

00:39:11.09 --> 00:39:12.73
So the parameters
of our model are now

00:39:12.73 --> 00:39:14.18
all the different
layers of weights

00:39:14.18 --> 00:39:17.02
and all the different
layers of biases.

00:39:17.02 --> 00:39:20.06
And so like we said, you
can do linear classification

00:39:20.06 --> 00:39:21.04
with a perceptron.

00:39:21.04 --> 00:39:23.02
But the fun thing is-- oops--

00:39:23.02 --> 00:39:25.66
you can do non-linear
classification with a deep net,

00:39:25.66 --> 00:39:29.38
even if that deep net is
just a two-layer perceptron.

00:39:29.38 --> 00:39:33.34
So here, now we've built a
double-layer model, still

00:39:33.34 --> 00:39:34.48
only in two dimensions.

00:39:34.48 --> 00:39:38.02
So now we have mapped
through essentially just

00:39:38.02 --> 00:39:40.50
this simple non-linearity
in between these two

00:39:40.50 --> 00:39:42.14
different layers.

00:39:42.14 --> 00:39:44.02
Now if we look at
what this looks like,

00:39:44.02 --> 00:39:48.50
that first layer is giving
us one kind of ramp.

00:39:48.50 --> 00:39:50.54
The second layer gives
us a different ramp

00:39:50.54 --> 00:39:52.34
because it's a
different linear model.

00:39:52.34 --> 00:39:55.38
And now if you do this
non-linear combination of them,

00:39:55.38 --> 00:39:58.74
essentially what you get out
is you can actually map--

00:39:58.74 --> 00:40:01.78
it's almost like think about
the intersection of one ramp

00:40:01.78 --> 00:40:03.14
with another ramp.

00:40:03.14 --> 00:40:07.46
You get a kind of triangular,
almost a pyramidal component

00:40:07.46 --> 00:40:10.52
that will be higher
than everything else.

00:40:10.52 --> 00:40:13.60
And so now you can still take
a simple threshold of that,

00:40:13.60 --> 00:40:16.23
and now you can get out a
non-linear classification.

00:40:16.23 --> 00:40:18.82
And, of course, you can expand
this into many more dimensions.

00:40:18.82 --> 00:40:21.68
But this is, I think, some
nice, simple intuition building

00:40:21.68 --> 00:40:25.64
as to why you start getting
non-linear models when you're

00:40:25.64 --> 00:40:30.60
taking linear combinations,
but with these non-linearities.

00:40:30.60 --> 00:40:31.32
Cool.

00:40:31.32 --> 00:40:34.52
So another-- so we expect
that you've seen a lot of that

00:40:34.52 --> 00:40:35.40
before.

00:40:35.40 --> 00:40:37.06
But moving forward,
one of the things

00:40:37.06 --> 00:40:38.52
we are going to
cover in this class

00:40:38.52 --> 00:40:41.12
is what we can approximate.

00:40:41.12 --> 00:40:44.08
So what do we know about
what is possible to

00:40:44.08 --> 00:40:47.00
approximate with deep learning?

00:40:47.00 --> 00:40:50.28
So one dimension of that
is representational power.

00:40:50.28 --> 00:40:52.00
So you have a single layer.

00:40:52.00 --> 00:40:55.80
You can do any linear
decision surface.

00:40:55.80 --> 00:40:57.80
If you have 2-plus
layers, in theory,

00:40:57.80 --> 00:41:01.28
you can actually
represent any function.

00:41:01.28 --> 00:41:03.28
And this is assuming,
of course, that you

00:41:03.28 --> 00:41:05.04
have some non-trivial
non-linearity

00:41:05.04 --> 00:41:06.89
between those layers
because if you don't

00:41:06.89 --> 00:41:08.44
have that non-linearity,
it turns out

00:41:08.44 --> 00:41:11.52
that a linear layer and
another linear layer, this

00:41:11.52 --> 00:41:13.13
is just a linear combination.

00:41:13.13 --> 00:41:14.21
It's still linear.

00:41:14.21 --> 00:41:16.13
So you need that non-linearity.

00:41:16.13 --> 00:41:18.75
But doing that, you can
actually approximate anything.

00:41:18.75 --> 00:41:20.21
And one way to
think about it is,

00:41:20.21 --> 00:41:24.97
given some amount of
capacity, some number

00:41:24.97 --> 00:41:28.35
of different dimensions
that you can do,

00:41:28.35 --> 00:41:30.47
you can actually
approximate any function.

00:41:30.47 --> 00:41:35.39
This is a rough,
hand-wavy intuition thing.

00:41:35.39 --> 00:41:44.84
But as you're shrinking the
size of these different vertical

00:41:44.84 --> 00:41:47.01
things, you're getting
something like a Riemann sum.

00:41:47.01 --> 00:41:48.61
You can approximate
any integral as long

00:41:48.61 --> 00:41:50.11
as you have enough
capacity in terms

00:41:50.11 --> 00:41:52.89
of the number of small things
that you're building up.

00:41:52.89 --> 00:41:57.45
So this gets at basically one of
the issues, which is efficiency.

00:41:57.45 --> 00:41:59.13
So in theory, you
could approximate

00:41:59.13 --> 00:42:03.49
any complicated function
with a sufficiently wide

00:42:03.49 --> 00:42:04.55
two-layer network.

00:42:04.55 --> 00:42:06.97
So this means not two
dimensions, maybe thousands

00:42:06.97 --> 00:42:09.33
or millions of dimensions.

00:42:09.33 --> 00:42:13.66
But in practice, that's
actually very inefficient.

00:42:13.66 --> 00:42:16.42
So a narrow deep
model, maybe something

00:42:16.42 --> 00:42:19.38
that only has much fewer
dimensions in terms

00:42:19.38 --> 00:42:22.36
of the size of each vector
or each input and output,

00:42:22.36 --> 00:42:25.42
but now many, many, many
more stacked layers,

00:42:25.42 --> 00:42:27.76
you might be able to, with
a lot less parameters,

00:42:27.76 --> 00:42:30.54
approximate the same
complex function.

00:42:30.54 --> 00:42:32.08
In practice, we do
find that's true.

00:42:32.08 --> 00:42:34.13
More layers helps.

00:42:34.13 --> 00:42:36.22
And we'll get into this a
little bit in lecture 3,

00:42:36.22 --> 00:42:39.06
which is about approximation
theory, the theory of what

00:42:39.06 --> 00:42:41.48
it is possible to approximate.

00:42:41.48 --> 00:42:42.94
And then the other
thing that we're

00:42:42.94 --> 00:42:45.42
going to cover in this
class is architectures.

00:42:45.42 --> 00:42:47.64
So when we talk
about deep networks,

00:42:47.64 --> 00:42:50.42
really, often, we're talking
about something that's roughly

00:42:50.42 --> 00:42:54.52
like this, some cascade of
repeated simple computations,

00:42:54.52 --> 00:42:56.86
each of these linear
and then linear models

00:42:56.86 --> 00:42:58.74
with a non-linearity.

00:42:58.74 --> 00:43:00.54
And then with every
increasing layer,

00:43:00.54 --> 00:43:03.12
we're getting more
abstracted representations.

00:43:03.12 --> 00:43:05.74
You're getting a
higher capacity model.

00:43:05.74 --> 00:43:08.38
And this means it can form
more complex and abstract

00:43:08.38 --> 00:43:09.62
calculations.

00:43:09.62 --> 00:43:13.66
Whereas a shallow network, if
it's not sufficiently wide,

00:43:13.66 --> 00:43:16.80
it could maybe only efficiently
compute simple things

00:43:16.80 --> 00:43:19.13
like maybe finding
edges, a deep network

00:43:19.13 --> 00:43:20.76
could efficiently
compute harder things

00:43:20.76 --> 00:43:24.64
like recognizing this clownfish
or translating English

00:43:24.64 --> 00:43:27.08
to Chinese.

00:43:27.08 --> 00:43:29.76
And so this classification
of complex things,

00:43:29.76 --> 00:43:31.36
essentially, your
whole function f

00:43:31.36 --> 00:43:36.44
of x is now this stacked
or almost chain-like

00:43:36.44 --> 00:43:39.28
mapping from your final
layer, which is then

00:43:39.28 --> 00:43:42.40
a functional mapping
of your previous layer,

00:43:42.40 --> 00:43:46.16
and in and in and in and in,
all the way back to that input.

00:43:46.16 --> 00:43:47.72
And there's a lot
of different ways

00:43:47.72 --> 00:43:49.68
that we've learned to
build these mappings

00:43:49.68 --> 00:43:52.08
for different types
of problems and based

00:43:52.08 --> 00:43:54.26
on the structure of the
data we're trying to model.

00:43:54.26 --> 00:43:55.26
And we'll get into that.

00:43:55.26 --> 00:43:58.54
We'll talk about CNNs, GNNs,
transformers, and RNNs,

00:43:58.54 --> 00:44:01.44
so convolutional neural
networks, graph neural networks,

00:44:01.44 --> 00:44:05.18
transformers, and
recurrent neural networks.

00:44:05.18 --> 00:44:06.64
And then the other
thing that we're

00:44:06.64 --> 00:44:08.26
going to be talking
about in this class

00:44:08.26 --> 00:44:12.25
is when and why
we can generalize.

00:44:12.25 --> 00:44:13.95
So why do deep nets generalize?

00:44:13.95 --> 00:44:15.93
Essentially, deep nets
have so many parameters,

00:44:15.93 --> 00:44:18.19
they could just act
like lookup tables.

00:44:18.19 --> 00:44:20.35
They would just regurgitate
the training data.

00:44:20.35 --> 00:44:23.07
But instead, they seem to
learn rules that generalize.

00:44:23.07 --> 00:44:25.11
And this actually
defies classical theory.

00:44:25.11 --> 00:44:27.85
So the classical
theory basically

00:44:27.85 --> 00:44:31.71
says that if your model
is overparameterized,

00:44:31.71 --> 00:44:33.03
it will overfit.

00:44:33.03 --> 00:44:37.57
There's this capacity
versus risk or error curve

00:44:37.57 --> 00:44:40.07
where if you don't
have enough capacity,

00:44:40.07 --> 00:44:41.77
you're going to
underfit the model.

00:44:41.77 --> 00:44:44.81
And then with too much capacity,
you're going to overfit.

00:44:44.81 --> 00:44:47.57
But in practice-- and this
is from this paper "Double

00:44:47.57 --> 00:44:48.85
Descent"--

00:44:48.85 --> 00:44:53.61
what we see is that in this
modern interpolating regime,

00:44:53.61 --> 00:44:56.51
we go past that point of
being overparameterized,

00:44:56.51 --> 00:44:59.33
and we're actually then
able to actually pass

00:44:59.33 --> 00:45:01.45
some interpolation
threshold or ability

00:45:01.45 --> 00:45:03.09
to have enough
capacity in the model

00:45:03.09 --> 00:45:05.27
to interpolate between
the data points.

00:45:05.27 --> 00:45:08.46
We're actually able to then
potentially get even better,

00:45:08.46 --> 00:45:10.76
even though the models are
massively overparameterized.

00:45:10.76 --> 00:45:11.35
Yeah.

00:45:11.35 --> 00:45:12.98
AUDIENCE: What do
you mean by capacity?

00:45:12.98 --> 00:45:13.98
SARA BEERY: Capacity
is the number

00:45:13.98 --> 00:45:15.44
of parameters in the model.

00:45:15.44 --> 00:45:17.38
So that's both the
width and the depth,

00:45:17.38 --> 00:45:22.14
basically how many values are
captured in those model weights?

00:45:22.14 --> 00:45:23.50
Yeah.

00:45:23.50 --> 00:45:26.18
Yeah.

00:45:26.18 --> 00:45:27.90
Yes, thank you.

00:45:27.90 --> 00:45:31.42
AUDIENCE: And how does that
relate to the size of the data

00:45:31.42 --> 00:45:33.14
samples that you're passing?

00:45:33.14 --> 00:45:34.58
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

00:45:34.58 --> 00:45:38.24
So that is not a
dimension on this plot,

00:45:38.24 --> 00:45:39.92
but it definitely
is also related.

00:45:39.92 --> 00:45:42.98
So if you have--
but it's related

00:45:42.98 --> 00:45:44.50
in a different dimension.

00:45:44.50 --> 00:45:47.42
If you're learning
from fewer data points,

00:45:47.42 --> 00:45:51.76
it's very easy to learn to maybe
overfit to those data points.

00:45:51.76 --> 00:45:54.76
So you could think of that
honestly, in another way,

00:45:54.76 --> 00:45:57.10
maybe as being a different
dimension of capacity here,

00:45:57.10 --> 00:45:59.72
where it's like almost the
capacity of your training data.

00:45:59.72 --> 00:46:02.40
But, of course, there's
not a direct translation.

00:46:02.40 --> 00:46:04.32
But so if you don't have
enough training data,

00:46:04.32 --> 00:46:06.57
you also can't learn to
interpolate between those data

00:46:06.57 --> 00:46:09.96
points because you don't have
enough coverage of the data

00:46:09.96 --> 00:46:12.25
distribution.

00:46:12.25 --> 00:46:13.38
I live in that world a lot.

00:46:13.38 --> 00:46:16.68
We often don't have
enough data to learn

00:46:16.68 --> 00:46:19.82
a general representation
without a lot of handy tricks.

00:46:19.82 --> 00:46:22.00
We will talk about some
of those different tricks

00:46:22.00 --> 00:46:23.70
in this class, some
of the different ways

00:46:23.70 --> 00:46:26.80
that we try to convince
models not to overfit to data

00:46:26.80 --> 00:46:30.56
and try to learn to
generalize beyond maybe what

00:46:30.56 --> 00:46:31.99
is captured in the data.

00:46:31.99 --> 00:46:33.95
But I think that dimension
is really important,

00:46:33.95 --> 00:46:36.24
and it's one of the
reasons that none of this

00:46:36.24 --> 00:46:39.16
was possible until we started
building big enough data sets,

00:46:39.16 --> 00:46:41.36
where you had enough
coverage of the space

00:46:41.36 --> 00:46:43.52
that you wanted to
model in your data

00:46:43.52 --> 00:46:46.46
to be able to start
interpolating between them.

00:46:46.46 --> 00:46:46.96
Yeah.

00:46:46.96 --> 00:46:48.83
AUDIENCE: Can you just
clarify the difference

00:46:48.83 --> 00:46:51.52
between overfitting
and overparameterized?

00:46:51.52 --> 00:46:54.42
SARA BEERY: Oh, yeah.

00:46:54.42 --> 00:46:55.92
So, I mean, I think
this paper would

00:46:55.92 --> 00:46:58.22
be great to read to be
able to really get into it.

00:46:58.22 --> 00:47:00.72
But essentially,
the idea here is

00:47:00.72 --> 00:47:02.28
that in the previous
literature, we

00:47:02.28 --> 00:47:06.27
said that at some point, if
you had too many parameters,

00:47:06.27 --> 00:47:09.45
you were going to overfit and
learn a model that actually

00:47:09.45 --> 00:47:12.49
generalized worse because based
on the amount of data that you

00:47:12.49 --> 00:47:17.01
had, it was going to-- there's a
classical example where you have

00:47:17.01 --> 00:47:18.51
three data points.

00:47:18.51 --> 00:47:23.51
And if you try to learn a
12-dimensional function,

00:47:23.51 --> 00:47:25.93
it will learn something like
this to fit those data points

00:47:25.93 --> 00:47:26.51
perfectly.

00:47:26.51 --> 00:47:28.69
But actually, it's not
likely to generalize

00:47:28.69 --> 00:47:32.17
because it's basically
too spiky or too peaky.

00:47:32.17 --> 00:47:34.25
So that's the idea
of overfitting.

00:47:34.25 --> 00:47:36.37
But this idea of
overparameterized

00:47:36.37 --> 00:47:39.45
is basically instead of saying
underfitting and overfitting,

00:47:39.45 --> 00:47:42.09
now we're saying that the new
paradigm is underparametrized

00:47:42.09 --> 00:47:43.63
and overparameterized.

00:47:43.63 --> 00:47:45.41
And what they're
basically saying

00:47:45.41 --> 00:47:50.37
is that you maybe can't
be too overparameterized,

00:47:50.37 --> 00:47:53.57
though this perspective
is one that doesn't

00:47:53.57 --> 00:47:56.01
take into account resources.

00:47:56.01 --> 00:48:00.37
So there's many, many
people and places and places

00:48:00.37 --> 00:48:02.97
that are at MIT, but even
actually in this class,

00:48:02.97 --> 00:48:05.24
where we don't have
infinite compute resources.

00:48:05.24 --> 00:48:06.62
So actually, what
you really want

00:48:06.62 --> 00:48:08.82
is some optimal point
on this curve where

00:48:08.82 --> 00:48:12.38
you get good performance,
but you're not maybe

00:48:12.38 --> 00:48:14.14
using resources
that you don't have,

00:48:14.14 --> 00:48:17.46
super, super large memory GPUs.

00:48:17.46 --> 00:48:19.26
Yeah?

00:48:19.26 --> 00:48:21.10
AUDIENCE: When you gave
the example of using

00:48:21.10 --> 00:48:24.86
two very wide layers versus
multiple narrow ones,

00:48:24.86 --> 00:48:27.68
[INAUDIBLE] there's
a rule of thumb about

00:48:27.68 --> 00:48:30.42
which is better for about
the interpretability

00:48:30.42 --> 00:48:31.70
between the two?

00:48:31.70 --> 00:48:33.26
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

00:48:33.26 --> 00:48:35.10
OK, we're definitely
going to get

00:48:35.10 --> 00:48:38.26
into this a lot more in
some of the later lectures,

00:48:38.26 --> 00:48:44.50
these choices between breadth
versus depth in models.

00:48:44.50 --> 00:48:47.92
But at a very high
level, your intuition,

00:48:47.92 --> 00:48:50.58
I think, that you're
getting at is correct.

00:48:50.58 --> 00:48:54.60
If you have only two layers--

00:48:54.60 --> 00:48:56.06
actually, maybe
not, because if you

00:48:56.06 --> 00:48:59.08
have two almost infinite
parameter layers,

00:48:59.08 --> 00:49:02.54
really, really high dimensional
space is hard to interpret.

00:49:02.54 --> 00:49:06.60
If you have lower dimensional
space over many, many different

00:49:06.60 --> 00:49:09.58
now stacked versions
of these layers,

00:49:09.58 --> 00:49:11.14
it's also hard to interpret.

00:49:11.14 --> 00:49:14.84
So I would say like in ecology,
for example, people often

00:49:14.84 --> 00:49:16.52
still use just
generative additive

00:49:16.52 --> 00:49:18.57
models of a bunch of
different linear things

00:49:18.57 --> 00:49:20.20
because they need
the interpretability,

00:49:20.20 --> 00:49:22.68
or they feel like they do.

00:49:22.68 --> 00:49:25.92
And anytime you get
to larger numbers

00:49:25.92 --> 00:49:29.00
of parameters in either
dimension, that interpretability

00:49:29.00 --> 00:49:31.26
gets more difficult.

00:49:31.26 --> 00:49:32.64
AUDIENCE: I was
just going to add

00:49:32.64 --> 00:49:38.44
that even in, let's say OpenAI,
it's a closely guarded secret

00:49:38.44 --> 00:49:40.72
what recipe of
width versus depth

00:49:40.72 --> 00:49:43.76
that they use in GPT whatever.

00:49:43.76 --> 00:49:46.00
If you can come up with
a really scientific way

00:49:46.00 --> 00:49:48.24
to answer that question,
you could go and get

00:49:48.24 --> 00:49:49.04
a job there and--

00:49:49.04 --> 00:49:50.66
SARA BEERY: Make 7 figures.

00:49:50.66 --> 00:49:51.60
[LAUGHS]

00:49:51.60 --> 00:49:52.60
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:52.60 --> 00:49:54.52
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so
what Jeremy is saying is,

00:49:54.52 --> 00:49:58.08
there's no perfect prescription
or recipe for width versus depth

00:49:58.08 --> 00:49:59.52
and what's optimal.

00:49:59.52 --> 00:50:02.01
Yeah.

00:50:02.01 --> 00:50:05.41
So the simplicity hypothesis
in classical theory

00:50:05.41 --> 00:50:08.57
basically says that big models
learn complicated functions

00:50:08.57 --> 00:50:09.99
and overfit the data.

00:50:09.99 --> 00:50:13.45
And the emerging theory is
that deep networks actually

00:50:13.45 --> 00:50:16.02
learn simple functions
that generalize.

00:50:16.02 --> 00:50:18.61
And we're going to talk about
this a lot more in a theoretical

00:50:18.61 --> 00:50:21.51
and a more experimental
lecture around generalization,

00:50:21.51 --> 00:50:24.57
both in and out of distribution.

00:50:24.57 --> 00:50:26.49
So again, what I've
expected you to see

00:50:26.49 --> 00:50:29.97
before-- softmax,
cross-entropy loss-- basically,

00:50:29.97 --> 00:50:32.22
we have this model
that we're trying

00:50:32.22 --> 00:50:34.43
to use to learn to classify
something like clownfish.

00:50:34.43 --> 00:50:37.77
And we assume that we've seen
some of these really basic loss

00:50:37.77 --> 00:50:39.07
functions that we use for this.

00:50:39.07 --> 00:50:43.21
So for example, if you have
this as your last layer,

00:50:43.21 --> 00:50:45.01
and now you have some
set of categories

00:50:45.01 --> 00:50:47.37
you're trying to learn
to categorize over,

00:50:47.37 --> 00:50:49.77
the simple version of this
is you take the argmax,

00:50:49.77 --> 00:50:52.49
and you say, OK, well, the
thing that's darkest on here

00:50:52.49 --> 00:50:54.45
is clownfish.

00:50:54.45 --> 00:50:57.13
And then the loss that
you would calculate

00:50:57.13 --> 00:51:01.60
is, OK, the actual correct
ground truth label is clownfish,

00:51:01.60 --> 00:51:04.46
and then the error between those
two, in this case, is zero.

00:51:04.46 --> 00:51:05.40
So it's small.

00:51:05.40 --> 00:51:08.86
So you've gotten the
correct prediction.

00:51:08.86 --> 00:51:12.30
And then maybe, actually,
the real ground truth label

00:51:12.30 --> 00:51:14.71
is grizzly bear, and
you've said clownfish.

00:51:14.71 --> 00:51:16.76
Now what you'd want is
for your loss to be large.

00:51:16.76 --> 00:51:18.58
So you want some
loss function that

00:51:18.58 --> 00:51:21.34
does a good job of punishing
you when you're wrong

00:51:21.34 --> 00:51:24.66
and not punishing you
when you're right.

00:51:24.66 --> 00:51:26.62
And the way we
actually do this often

00:51:26.62 --> 00:51:31.32
is if you assume the net output
is some normalized vector,

00:51:31.32 --> 00:51:33.38
then you can think of
this as a probability

00:51:33.38 --> 00:51:37.30
distribution over different
possible object classes.

00:51:37.30 --> 00:51:39.72
And so what you want this
loss function on the right,

00:51:39.72 --> 00:51:42.02
this is called
cross-entropy loss.

00:51:42.02 --> 00:51:43.98
This essentially
indicates the distance

00:51:43.98 --> 00:51:47.74
between what the model believes
the output distribution should

00:51:47.74 --> 00:51:50.76
be and what the input
distribution actually is,

00:51:50.76 --> 00:51:53.84
which in this case is what
we call a one hot encoding,

00:51:53.84 --> 00:51:56.86
or essentially a vector that's 0
everywhere except for the place

00:51:56.86 --> 00:51:59.23
where it's correct,
where it's 1.

00:51:59.23 --> 00:52:01.28
And so essentially, it's
just telling the network

00:52:01.28 --> 00:52:04.40
to maximize the probability
of the training data, which

00:52:04.40 --> 00:52:07.20
forces the output to be a
reasonably good probability

00:52:07.20 --> 00:52:09.94
model of the object
class, given the image,

00:52:09.94 --> 00:52:12.28
assuming you have
enough training data.

00:52:12.28 --> 00:52:15.14
So what this looks like in
practice, you say clownfish,

00:52:15.14 --> 00:52:17.96
the ground truth
label is clownfish.

00:52:17.96 --> 00:52:22.60
Maybe this is your
prediction, so now the score

00:52:22.60 --> 00:52:24.72
is basically saying,
this is how much better

00:52:24.72 --> 00:52:27.04
you could have done--

00:52:27.04 --> 00:52:28.74
how much better you
could have done,

00:52:28.74 --> 00:52:31.76
which is the difference between
the amount of probability

00:52:31.76 --> 00:52:33.00
you gave the class clownfish.

00:52:33.00 --> 00:52:34.42
I'm putting probability
in quotes here.

00:52:34.42 --> 00:52:35.88
It's super important to
know that these are not

00:52:35.88 --> 00:52:36.90
true probabilities.

00:52:36.90 --> 00:52:39.36
They're scores.

00:52:39.36 --> 00:52:42.36
And then that red
is the dimension

00:52:42.36 --> 00:52:43.47
that you could do better.

00:52:43.47 --> 00:52:45.64
And that's telling you the
direction of the gradient

00:52:45.64 --> 00:52:47.96
you want to go in.

00:52:47.96 --> 00:52:50.78
So maybe grizzly bear,
you got it correct.

00:52:50.78 --> 00:52:51.62
It's grizzly bear.

00:52:51.62 --> 00:52:58.00
Now the amount that's left
over is small versus actually

00:52:58.00 --> 00:53:02.48
if it's chameleon, and
you're saying iguana.

00:53:02.48 --> 00:53:04.07
Essentially, this
thing is normalized,

00:53:04.07 --> 00:53:06.33
so if you put a lot more
probability on one thing, then

00:53:06.33 --> 00:53:07.78
the correct answer,
then you end up

00:53:07.78 --> 00:53:10.33
with a much smaller amount
that now you're looking

00:53:10.33 --> 00:53:12.81
at the difference between it.

00:53:12.81 --> 00:53:16.17
And basically, the idea with
all of this in deep learning

00:53:16.17 --> 00:53:18.73
is you're taking all
these different weights

00:53:18.73 --> 00:53:23.17
in your massive model, and then
you're fiddling around with them

00:53:23.17 --> 00:53:25.17
until you match the
desired output for all

00:53:25.17 --> 00:53:26.41
your training data.

00:53:26.41 --> 00:53:29.65
You're basically just
wiggling around all

00:53:29.65 --> 00:53:30.81
these different weights.

00:53:30.81 --> 00:53:33.39
So you're going through
your training data,

00:53:33.39 --> 00:53:36.25
you're predicting something,
you're calculating a loss,

00:53:36.25 --> 00:53:38.99
and you're doing this for
many different data samples.

00:53:38.99 --> 00:53:40.37
And you're doing
it over and over

00:53:40.37 --> 00:53:42.33
and over again until the
model gets everything

00:53:42.33 --> 00:53:46.93
right, or as close to
everything right as possible.

00:53:46.93 --> 00:53:48.81
We also expect that
you've seen some amount

00:53:48.81 --> 00:53:52.37
of parallel processing and the
idea of tensors before this.

00:53:52.37 --> 00:53:54.57
So this is the idea that
a lot of this stuff,

00:53:54.57 --> 00:53:56.95
because it's repeated
calculations,

00:53:56.95 --> 00:53:58.31
you can be done in batch.

00:53:58.31 --> 00:54:01.43
So each of these individual
losses for the individual data

00:54:01.43 --> 00:54:03.72
points, well, it's all
going to be summed anyway.

00:54:03.72 --> 00:54:05.43
So you can do it in
parallel, and now you

00:54:05.43 --> 00:54:09.95
can take these batches and more
efficiently calculate that sum.

00:54:09.95 --> 00:54:13.95
And so look, you take the same
layer for all three of these,

00:54:13.95 --> 00:54:16.31
you can stack them,
and now essentially you

00:54:16.31 --> 00:54:20.47
get this matrix that's
features multiplied by images.

00:54:20.47 --> 00:54:22.63
And so that's what
your tensors are.

00:54:22.63 --> 00:54:24.55
They're these
multi-dimensional arrays,

00:54:24.55 --> 00:54:26.91
and every single layer
is some representation

00:54:26.91 --> 00:54:29.23
of the input data.

00:54:29.23 --> 00:54:31.09
And actually,
everything is a tensor.

00:54:31.09 --> 00:54:33.47
A tensor is just a
multi-dimensional matrix.

00:54:33.47 --> 00:54:36.99
So you can say, OK, here's our
mathematical representation

00:54:36.99 --> 00:54:38.19
of this model.

00:54:38.19 --> 00:54:40.81
But you can also just
represent it this way.

00:54:40.81 --> 00:54:44.65
You have these vectors of
your different input values,

00:54:44.65 --> 00:54:46.33
and now you have
your batch dimension,

00:54:46.33 --> 00:54:49.61
which is now this vertical
stacking of all those layers.

00:54:49.61 --> 00:54:52.07
And now you multiply that
by your weights matrix,

00:54:52.07 --> 00:54:53.79
and you get out your
output, and then

00:54:53.79 --> 00:54:55.81
you run that through a
pointwise non-linearity,

00:54:55.81 --> 00:54:58.46
and then again, just another
matrix multiplication.

00:54:58.46 --> 00:55:00.48
And then you get your
output z2, and then you

00:55:00.48 --> 00:55:02.32
take that non-linearity,
and you get y.

00:55:02.32 --> 00:55:05.48
So this is why
the fact that GPUs

00:55:05.48 --> 00:55:07.48
can do so many
multiplications in parallel

00:55:07.48 --> 00:55:10.64
was important, because we can
rewrite all of these models

00:55:10.64 --> 00:55:13.14
as just essentially
sets of multiplications.

00:55:13.14 --> 00:55:15.04
And we'll go into this
in a lot more detail

00:55:15.04 --> 00:55:16.49
as well in the future.

00:55:16.49 --> 00:55:18.24
So what we're going
to cover in this class

00:55:18.24 --> 00:55:20.74
is how deep networks
represent data.

00:55:20.74 --> 00:55:29.12
So essentially, it's
this idea, where

00:55:29.12 --> 00:55:33.28
deep networks are a more compact
way of representing knowledge

00:55:33.28 --> 00:55:34.40
in data.

00:55:34.40 --> 00:55:39.36
And previous methods we saw
were more like lookup tables.

00:55:39.36 --> 00:55:41.16
But there's other
and maybe better ways

00:55:41.16 --> 00:55:43.40
to learn a mapping
between input and output

00:55:43.40 --> 00:55:47.64
that basically assume that there
are these lower-level building

00:55:47.64 --> 00:55:50.88
blocks that are useful for many
different possible downstream

00:55:50.88 --> 00:55:51.68
tasks.

00:55:51.68 --> 00:55:53.88
So one nice example
is the idea of trying

00:55:53.88 --> 00:55:56.29
to learn a classifier
for the letter T.

00:55:56.29 --> 00:55:58.47
So if you want to learn
to classify the letter T,

00:55:58.47 --> 00:56:00.76
you could try to build a
classifier just for the letter

00:56:00.76 --> 00:56:03.69
T. Or you could take a
classifier that classifies

00:56:03.69 --> 00:56:06.45
lines, and you could
say, OK, here's a line,

00:56:06.45 --> 00:56:09.09
and then you could rotate, have
a rotation of that classifier,

00:56:09.09 --> 00:56:10.53
and you'd say, here's a line.

00:56:10.53 --> 00:56:13.41
And now all you have to do is
understand the connection point

00:56:13.41 --> 00:56:14.55
between these two lines.

00:56:14.55 --> 00:56:19.77
So that line classifier is
reusable in some combination

00:56:19.77 --> 00:56:21.39
to get a T classifier.

00:56:21.39 --> 00:56:22.83
And that's the basic idea here.

00:56:22.83 --> 00:56:27.55
And actually in
this post hoc way,

00:56:27.55 --> 00:56:29.91
we've analyzed what
is learned in layers

00:56:29.91 --> 00:56:31.79
of things like convolutional
neural networks.

00:56:31.79 --> 00:56:34.69
And we do see that you'll
get low-level representations

00:56:34.69 --> 00:56:35.87
in the earlier layers.

00:56:35.87 --> 00:56:38.33
Then they get combined
into more and more complex

00:56:38.33 --> 00:56:42.29
representations in
those later layers.

00:56:42.29 --> 00:56:44.15
And you can actually
see this as well.

00:56:44.15 --> 00:56:48.17
If you take an input
image, and you take those,

00:56:48.17 --> 00:56:52.65
and you cluster them in some
low-level representation

00:56:52.65 --> 00:56:56.00
of the space for each layer, the
layers earlier on in the network

00:56:56.00 --> 00:56:58.71
are not going to cluster well by
category, because they're really

00:56:58.71 --> 00:57:00.35
just categorizing
things like, does it

00:57:00.35 --> 00:57:04.55
have directional orientations
of light and dark gradients

00:57:04.55 --> 00:57:05.71
in the pixels?

00:57:05.71 --> 00:57:08.21
But actually, when you get
towards the end of the network,

00:57:08.21 --> 00:57:09.91
it's learning more
concepts, and that's

00:57:09.91 --> 00:57:12.32
where you start getting these
clusters of different things

00:57:12.32 --> 00:57:14.55
like fish.

00:57:14.55 --> 00:57:19.75
And we will talk about actually
how that representation learns

00:57:19.75 --> 00:57:23.39
and is structured through space
in a bunch of different lectures

00:57:23.39 --> 00:57:25.43
on representation learning,
where we talk about

00:57:25.43 --> 00:57:29.65
reconstruction,
similarity, and theory.

00:57:29.65 --> 00:57:32.03
We're also going to cover
generative models going forward

00:57:32.03 --> 00:57:33.35
in the class.

00:57:33.35 --> 00:57:35.67
And this is things like
text-based and image-based

00:57:35.67 --> 00:57:37.07
generation.

00:57:37.07 --> 00:57:39.47
And we'll cover basics,
representations,

00:57:39.47 --> 00:57:42.67
and conditional models.

00:57:42.67 --> 00:57:44.59
And then we're going
to cover in this class

00:57:44.59 --> 00:57:46.87
how to think about
weight reuse and some

00:57:46.87 --> 00:57:48.67
of the efficiencies
in terms of training

00:57:48.67 --> 00:57:50.63
if you're able to
reuse components

00:57:50.63 --> 00:57:53.80
of previously trained models.

00:57:53.80 --> 00:57:57.38
So here, again, this
idea that, all right,

00:57:57.38 --> 00:58:02.32
well, you learned this thing to
categorize pictures of animals,

00:58:02.32 --> 00:58:04.56
and now maybe you
want to categorize

00:58:04.56 --> 00:58:06.62
some sort of satellite
imagery, well,

00:58:06.62 --> 00:58:10.36
actually, a lot of those
initial components about lines

00:58:10.36 --> 00:58:13.00
and orientations and
structures, those

00:58:13.00 --> 00:58:14.62
are useful for both of these.

00:58:14.62 --> 00:58:19.32
So what do we really need to
learn from scratch versus what

00:58:19.32 --> 00:58:22.16
is generally useful in terms
of the representations that

00:58:22.16 --> 00:58:24.34
are learned?

00:58:24.34 --> 00:58:25.76
And this is really
valuable if you

00:58:25.76 --> 00:58:28.18
don't have big data
or big compute,

00:58:28.18 --> 00:58:31.24
if you can pre-generate
representations that then you

00:58:31.24 --> 00:58:34.32
can learn on top of without
needing to learn everything

00:58:34.32 --> 00:58:36.92
from scratch.

00:58:36.92 --> 00:58:39.64
And so we'll get
into whether or not

00:58:39.64 --> 00:58:42.38
these things are
generalizable or transferable

00:58:42.38 --> 00:58:43.88
in these transfer
learning lectures,

00:58:43.88 --> 00:58:46.40
where we talk about
models and data.

00:58:46.40 --> 00:58:49.80
And then finally, we'll
talk about scaling.

00:58:49.80 --> 00:58:53.89
So when you think about
the scale of a brain,

00:58:53.89 --> 00:58:57.83
something like this little
worm only has 302 neurons,

00:58:57.83 --> 00:59:02.65
so you can think of that
as 302 different values

00:59:02.65 --> 00:59:03.85
in its neural network.

00:59:03.85 --> 00:59:06.57
A fruit fly is 15,000.

00:59:06.57 --> 00:59:09.97
Human beings have
100 billion, roughly.

00:59:09.97 --> 00:59:13.11
Elephants have 250 billion
neurons in their brains.

00:59:13.11 --> 00:59:14.57
I work with elephants a lot.

00:59:14.57 --> 00:59:15.59
They're amazing.

00:59:18.53 --> 00:59:21.67
And we'll talk about
scale and deep learning

00:59:21.67 --> 00:59:23.05
and what this
means, and actually

00:59:23.05 --> 00:59:24.45
get into some of the
really great questions

00:59:24.45 --> 00:59:26.13
that different people
asked in terms of,

00:59:26.13 --> 00:59:30.21
how does maybe scaling rules
change when you're thinking

00:59:30.21 --> 00:59:32.47
about optimization, what
are the laws of scaling,

00:59:32.47 --> 00:59:33.93
and how do we think
about something

00:59:33.93 --> 00:59:38.69
like automatically learning
how to best optimize a model?

00:59:38.69 --> 00:59:39.73
Great.

00:59:39.73 --> 00:59:43.29
So I tried to cover
today how we got

00:59:43.29 --> 00:59:46.79
where we are today, basically
just honestly mostly joking,

00:59:46.79 --> 00:59:49.81
but definitely a brief history
of these fluctuation and hype

00:59:49.81 --> 00:59:50.89
cycles.

00:59:50.89 --> 00:59:53.95
I would say that
everyone's pretty clear

00:59:53.95 --> 00:59:58.67
that we're currently in a really
hypey part of the hype cycle.

00:59:58.67 --> 01:00:03.61
What we expected you maybe saw
before and also very high level,

01:00:03.61 --> 01:00:06.67
some of the things we're
going to cover in this class.

01:00:06.67 --> 01:00:11.11
So I'm happy to, if anyone
has any final questions--

01:00:11.11 --> 01:00:12.19
yeah.

01:00:12.19 --> 01:00:13.56
